addSubject({id:'bio', topics:[{"t": "I.1 Enzymes", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe enzymes as biological catalysts</li><li>explain the induced-fit model</li><li>explain effects of pH, temperature, surface area and concentration (optimum, specificity, denaturing)</li><li>plan an experiment on a factor affecting enzyme rate</li></ul></div><h3>1. What enzymes do</h3><p>Enzymes are <b>proteins</b> that act as <b>biological catalysts</b>: they speed up reactions in cells without being used up, by lowering the activation energy. Each enzyme works on one <b>substrate</b> (specificity), e.g. amylase breaks starch into maltose, protease breaks protein into amino acids, lipase breaks fats into fatty acids + glycerol.</p><h3>2. Induced-fit model</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 110\" font-size=\"11\"><path d=\"M20 90 L20 40 L45 40 L55 60 L75 60 L85 40 L110 40 L110 90 Z\" fill=\"#9fb6e3\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"25\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"105\">enzyme + substrate</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"18\">→</text><path d=\"M160 90 L160 40 L185 40 L192 52 L218 52 L225 40 L250 40 L250 90 Z\" fill=\"#9fb6e3\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><circle cx=\"205\" cy=\"45\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"170\" y=\"105\">complex (site moulds)</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"18\">→</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"58\">products</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The active site changes shape slightly to fit the substrate (induced fit)</div></div><p>The substrate binds to the <b>active site</b>, forming an enzyme–substrate complex. The active site is flexible and moulds around the substrate, putting strain on its bonds so it breaks (or joins) more easily. Products leave; the enzyme is reused.</p><h3>3. Factors affecting enzyme activity</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 140\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><path d=\"M40 110 Q120 100 160 30 Q175 10 190 40 L220 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"132\">temperature</text><text x=\"2\" y=\"12\">rate</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#c0392b\">optimum ~37 °C</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#c0392b\">denatured</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Rate rises with temperature to an optimum, then falls steeply as the enzyme denatures</div></div><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>more KE → more collisions up to the optimum (37 °C for humans); above it the active site changes shape (<b>denatured</b>, permanent)</td></tr><tr><td>pH</td><td>each enzyme has an optimum pH (pepsin ~2 in the stomach, amylase ~7); extreme pH denatures</td></tr><tr><td>Substrate concentration</td><td>rate rises then levels off when all active sites are busy</td></tr><tr><td>Enzyme concentration / surface area</td><td>more active sites → faster</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Exam link</b>Amylase + starch at different pH: time for the iodine colour to disappear is shortest at pH 7 (fastest), longest at pH 3. (KSSC 2024)</div><div class=\"warn\">⚠ Never say the enzyme is \"killed\" – enzymes are not alive. Say <b>denatured</b>.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Enzymes are specific because…", "o": ["they catalyse any reaction", "they are carbohydrates", "they work best when frozen", "each binds to a particular substrate"], "a": 3, "e": "Complementary active site."}, {"q": "At high substrate concentration the rate levels off because…", "o": ["the enzyme is denatured", "all active sites are occupied", "the substrate runs out", "the pH falls"], "a": 1, "e": "The enzyme is saturated."}, {"q": "Denaturation means…", "o": ["the enzyme speeds up", "the active site permanently changes shape", "the substrate changes", "the enzyme reproduces"], "a": 1, "e": "Caused by high temperature or extreme pH."}, {"q": "The induced fit model says the active site is…", "o": ["rigid", "flexible and moulds to the substrate", "absent", "made of DNA"], "a": 1, "e": "It changes shape slightly."}, {"q": "The optimum temperature of most human enzymes", "o": ["0 °C", "20 °C", "37 °C", "80 °C"], "a": 2, "e": "Body temperature."}]}, {"t": "I.2 Photosynthesis", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe photosynthesis as converting light energy to chemical energy</li><li>write the balanced equation</li><li>locate the light and dark reactions in the chloroplast</li><li>explain why there are two phases</li></ul></div><h3>1. The equation</h3><div class=\"key\">6CO₂ + 6H₂O  —light, chlorophyll→  C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</div><p>Light energy is trapped by <b>chlorophyll</b> and stored as chemical energy in glucose. Glucose is used for respiration, or stored as starch, or made into cellulose, proteins and oils.</p><h3>2. Two stages in the chloroplast</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 130\" font-size=\"11\"><ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"65\" rx=\"150\" ry=\"55\" fill=\"#dff0d8\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"40\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"50\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"105\" y=\"60\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"105\" y=\"70\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"40\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"50\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"195\" y=\"60\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"195\" y=\"70\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"240\" y=\"40\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><rect x=\"240\" y=\"50\" width=\"34\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"25\">grana (thylakoids): LIGHT reactions</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"110\">stroma: DARK reactions (Calvin cycle)</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The chloroplast</div></div><table><tr><th>Light reactions</th><th>Dark reactions (Calvin cycle)</th></tr><tr><td>in the <b>grana / thylakoids</b></td><td>in the <b>stroma</b></td></tr><tr><td>need light</td><td>do not need light directly</td></tr><tr><td>water is split → O₂ released; ATP and NADPH made</td><td>CO₂ is fixed into glucose using ATP and NADPH</td></tr></table><p>Two phases are needed because light energy must first be captured as ATP/NADPH, which then power the building of glucose from CO₂.</p><h3>3. Limiting factors</h3><p>Rate of photosynthesis is limited by light intensity, CO₂ concentration and temperature. Increasing a factor speeds it up until another factor becomes limiting (graph levels off).</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "In the light reactions, NADP⁺ is…", "o": ["split", "used to make ATP directly", "oxidised", "reduced to NADPH"], "a": 3, "e": "It accepts electrons and H⁺."}, {"q": "Where does the Calvin cycle take place?", "o": ["Thylakoid", "Stroma", "Mitochondria", "Nucleus"], "a": 1, "e": "The light reactions are in the thylakoids."}, {"q": "The gas released by photosynthesis comes from…", "o": ["CO₂", "water", "glucose", "chlorophyll"], "a": 1, "e": "Water is split in the light reactions."}, {"q": "Products of the light reactions used in the dark reactions", "o": ["O₂ and glucose", "ATP and NADPH", "CO₂ and water", "starch"], "a": 1, "e": "Energy carriers."}]}, {"t": "I.3 Respiration", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe respiration as making ATP</li><li>write the aerobic equation</li><li>locate glycolysis, Krebs cycle and electron transport (cytoplasm, matrix, cristae)</li><li>explain the role of oxygen; compare aerobic and anaerobic; applications (bread, brewing)</li></ul></div><h3>1. Aerobic respiration</h3><div class=\"key\">C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy (≈36–38 ATP)</div><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 120\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"40\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff3cd\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"18\" y=\"58\">Glycolysis</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"72\">cytoplasm, 2 ATP</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"62\">→</text><ellipse cx=\"225\" cy=\"60\" rx=\"110\" ry=\"50\" fill=\"#fde2e2\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M135 60 q12 -25 24 0 q12 25 24 0 q12 -25 24 0 q12 25 24 0 q12 -25 24 0 q12 25 24 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"30\">Krebs cycle: matrix, 2 ATP</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"100\">Electron transport: cristae,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"112\">≈32–34 ATP, needs O₂</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Stages of aerobic respiration</div></div><p>Oxygen is the <b>final electron acceptor</b> in the electron transport chain; it combines with H⁺ to make water. Without O₂ the chain stops and only glycolysis can make ATP.</p><h3>2. Anaerobic respiration</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>Animals (muscles)</th><th>Yeast / plants (fermentation)</th></tr><tr><td>Products</td><td>lactic acid</td><td>ethanol + CO₂</td></tr><tr><td>ATP</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Uses / effects</td><td>muscle fatigue, cramps, \"oxygen debt\"</td><td>bread rises (CO₂), toddy/beer (ethanol)</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Anaerobic respiration in animals produces…", "o": ["lactic acid", "ethanol and water", "CO₂ and water", "ethanol and CO₂"], "a": 0, "e": "Yeast makes ethanol + CO₂."}, {"q": "Word equation for aerobic respiration", "o": ["CO₂ + water → glucose + oxygen", "glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water", "glucose + CO₂ → water + oxygen", "glucose + water → CO₂ + oxygen"], "a": 1, "e": "Releases energy as ATP."}, {"q": "Yeast respiring without oxygen for 24 h produces…", "o": ["CO₂ + ethanol", "CO₂ + lactic acid", "O₂ + ethanol", "O₂ + lactic acid"], "a": 0, "e": "Fermentation."}, {"q": "The role of O₂ in the electron transport chain", "o": ["the first electron donor", "the final electron acceptor", "it makes glucose", "it splits water"], "a": 1, "e": "It forms water."}]}, {"t": "I.4 Transport across membranes", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>compare diffusion, facilitated diffusion, osmosis and active transport</li><li>relate them to gas exchange, kidneys, guard cells, phagocytosis</li><li>describe osmosis in plant and animal cells (hypotonic, isotonic, hypertonic)</li><li>explain how surface area to volume ratio affects diffusion</li></ul></div><h3>1. The four processes</h3><table><tr><th>Process</th><th>What moves</th><th>Gradient</th><th>Energy?</th></tr><tr><td>Diffusion</td><td>small molecules (O₂, CO₂)</td><td>high → low</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>Facilitated diffusion</td><td>glucose, ions via carrier/channel proteins</td><td>high → low</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>Osmosis</td><td><b>water</b> through a partially permeable membrane</td><td>dilute → concentrated solution</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>Active transport</td><td>ions, glucose against the gradient</td><td>low → high</td><td><b>yes (ATP)</b></td></tr></table><h3>2. Osmosis in cells</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 130\" font-size=\"10\"><ellipse cx=\"55\" cy=\"35\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"#f8c8c8\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><ellipse cx=\"165\" cy=\"35\" rx=\"24\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"#f8c8c8\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><ellipse cx=\"275\" cy=\"35\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#f8c8c8\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"65\" width=\"50\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#dff0d8\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"32\" y=\"67\" width=\"46\" height=\"31\" fill=\"#9cd39f\"/><rect x=\"140\" y=\"65\" width=\"50\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#dff0d8\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"145\" y=\"70\" width=\"40\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#9cd39f\"/><rect x=\"250\" y=\"65\" width=\"50\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#dff0d8\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"265\" y=\"75\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" fill=\"#9cd39f\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"10\">hypotonic (dilute)</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"10\">isotonic</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"10\">hypertonic (conc.)</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"118\">swells, may burst</text><text x=\"25\" y=\"128\">plant: turgid</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"118\">no change</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"118\">shrinks</text><text x=\"212\" y=\"128\">plant: plasmolysed</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Red blood cells (top) and plant cells (bottom) in different solutions</div></div><p>The cell wall stops plant cells bursting: they become <b>turgid</b> (firm), which supports the plant.</p><h3>3. Surface area to volume ratio</h3><p>Small cells have a <b>large SA:V ratio</b>, so diffusion reaches the centre quickly. As size increases, volume grows faster than surface area, so large organisms need exchange surfaces (lungs, gills, villi) and transport systems.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Cube 1 cm: SA = 6, V = 1, ratio 6:1. Cube 2 cm: SA = 24, V = 8, ratio 3:1.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Osmosis is the movement of…", "o": ["any particle", "water across a partially permeable membrane", "ions using ATP", "glucose only"], "a": 1, "e": "Water from high to low water potential."}, {"q": "Which process needs ATP?", "o": ["Diffusion", "Osmosis", "Facilitated diffusion", "Active transport"], "a": 3, "e": "It moves substances against the gradient."}, {"q": "A red blood cell in pure water will…", "o": ["shrink", "burst", "stay the same", "divide"], "a": 1, "e": "Hypotonic: water enters and there is no wall."}, {"q": "Facilitated diffusion uses…", "o": ["ATP", "carrier/channel proteins", "the cell wall", "active sites"], "a": 1, "e": "Down the gradient, with no energy."}, {"q": "A plant cell in a hypertonic solution becomes…", "o": ["turgid", "plasmolysed", "burst", "larger"], "a": 1, "e": "Water leaves; the membrane pulls away from the wall."}]}, {"t": "I.5 Cell structure and the microscope", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>identify organelles and their functions</li><li>distinguish plant and animal cells</li><li>use a light microscope, find magnification and specimen size, make a wet mount</li></ul></div><h3>1. Plant and animal cells</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 140\" font-size=\"10\"><ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"65\" ry=\"55\" fill=\"#fde2e2\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"65\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#c9b6ef\" stroke=\"#7a4fc0\"/><ellipse cx=\"45\" cy=\"95\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"5\" fill=\"#f0a35e\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"135\">animal cell</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"15\" width=\"130\" height=\"110\" fill=\"#e6f4e2\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><rect x=\"195\" y=\"30\" width=\"80\" height=\"70\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#d8ecfb\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"292\" cy=\"40\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#c9b6ef\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"113\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><ellipse cx=\"225\" cy=\"113\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><ellipse cx=\"250\" cy=\"113\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><ellipse cx=\"275\" cy=\"113\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#3a9b4a\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"68\">large vacuole</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"138\">plant cell</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Plant cells have a cell wall, chloroplasts and a large permanent vacuole</div></div><table><tr><th>Organelle</th><th>Function</th></tr><tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>contains DNA, controls the cell</td></tr><tr><td>Mitochondrion</td><td>aerobic respiration → ATP</td></tr><tr><td>Chloroplast (plants)</td><td>photosynthesis</td></tr><tr><td>Ribosome</td><td>makes proteins</td></tr><tr><td>Rough ER / smooth ER</td><td>transports proteins / makes lipids</td></tr><tr><td>Golgi apparatus</td><td>packages and sends out proteins</td></tr><tr><td>Lysosome</td><td>digests worn-out parts and bacteria</td></tr><tr><td>Vacuole</td><td>stores cell sap, keeps the cell turgid</td></tr><tr><td>Cell membrane</td><td>controls what enters and leaves</td></tr><tr><td>Cell wall (plants)</td><td>cellulose; support, stops bursting</td></tr><tr><td>Centrioles (animals)</td><td>help in cell division</td></tr></table><h3>2. The light microscope</h3><div class=\"key\">Total magnification = eyepiece × objective<br>Actual size = image size ÷ magnification</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Field of view at ×400 is 0.4 mm and 8 cells fit across: each cell ≈ 0.4/8 = 0.05 mm = 50 µm.</div><p>Wet mount: put the specimen in a drop of water (or iodine/methylene blue stain) on a slide, lower a cover slip at an angle to avoid air bubbles. Start on low power, focus with the coarse knob, then switch to high power and use the fine knob.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Which organelle is found only in plant cells?", "o": ["Mitochondrion", "Nucleus", "Chloroplast", "Ribosome"], "a": 2, "e": "Also the cell wall and the large vacuole."}, {"q": "Where is most ATP made?", "o": ["Nucleus", "Mitochondria", "Ribosome", "Vacuole"], "a": 1, "e": "Aerobic respiration."}, {"q": "Why don't plant cells burst in a hypotonic solution?", "o": ["They have no vacuole", "The cellulose cell wall resists the pressure", "They lose water", "They have centrioles"], "a": 1, "e": "The wall stops the cell expanding further; the cell becomes turgid."}, {"q": "Proteins are made at the…", "o": ["ribosomes", "vacuole", "cell wall", "chloroplast"], "a": 0, "e": "Translation happens at the ribosomes."}, {"q": "Why do both plant and animal cells have mitochondria?", "o": ["To photosynthesise", "To make energy (ATP) by respiration", "To store water", "For support"], "a": 1, "e": "Every cell respires."}]}, {"t": "II.1 DNA and protein synthesis", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe DNA and RNA structure (sugar, phosphate, bases, base pairing)</li><li>relate gene → protein</li><li>outline transcription and translation (DNA, mRNA, tRNA, ribosome)</li></ul></div><h3>1. DNA and RNA</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 110\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"24\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">A</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">T</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"54\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">T</text><text x=\"54\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">A</text><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">G</text><text x=\"84\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">C</text><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"114\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">C</text><text x=\"114\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">G</text><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"144\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">A</text><text x=\"144\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">T</text><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"174\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">G</text><text x=\"174\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">C</text><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"204\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">C</text><text x=\"204\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">G</text><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"234\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">T</text><text x=\"234\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">A</text><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999\"/><text x=\"264\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#c0392b\">A</text><text x=\"264\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">T</text><path d=\"M15 20 L295 20\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M15 90 L295 90\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"108\">sugar–phosphate backbones; A–T, C–G pairs</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">DNA: a double helix (\"twisted ladder\")</div></div><table><tr><th></th><th>DNA</th><th>RNA</th></tr><tr><td>Sugar</td><td>deoxyribose</td><td>ribose</td></tr><tr><td>Strands</td><td>2 (double helix)</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Bases</td><td>A, T, C, G</td><td>A, <b>U</b>, C, G</td></tr><tr><td>Where</td><td>nucleus</td><td>nucleus and cytoplasm</td></tr></table><p>A <b>nucleotide</b> = sugar + phosphate + base. A <b>gene</b> is a section of DNA coding for one polypeptide (protein).</p><h3>2. Protein synthesis</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 90\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e6e0f8\" stroke=\"#7a4fc0\"/><text x=\"25\" y=\"44\">DNA (gene)</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"38\">transcription →</text><rect x=\"175\" y=\"20\" width=\"50\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff3cd\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"183\" y=\"44\">mRNA</text><text x=\"228\" y=\"38\">translation →</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"70\">protein</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"80\">in the nucleus</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"80\">at a ribosome</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Gene → mRNA → protein</div></div><ol><li><b>Transcription</b> (nucleus): the DNA unzips and a complementary mRNA copy of the gene is made.</li><li>mRNA leaves the nucleus to a <b>ribosome</b>.</li><li><b>Translation</b>: the ribosome reads the mRNA in triplets (codons); tRNA brings the matching amino acids, which are joined into a polypeptide chain.</li></ol><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>DNA strand TAC GGA → mRNA AUG CCU (swap T→A, A→U, C→G, G→C).</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Which base is NOT in DNA?", "o": ["Uracil", "Adenine", "Thymine", "Guanine"], "a": 0, "e": "Uracil replaces thymine in RNA."}, {"q": "The correct order in protein synthesis", "o": ["Replication → Translation → Folding", "Transcription → Translation → Folding", "Translation → Transcription → Folding", "Translation → Replication → Folding"], "a": 1, "e": "DNA → mRNA → protein."}, {"q": "A gene is…", "o": ["made of protein", "a section of DNA that codes for a protein", "a whole chromosome", "a cell"], "a": 1, "e": "Chromosomes carry many genes."}, {"q": "mRNA is made from DNA during…", "o": ["translation", "transcription", "replication", "meiosis"], "a": 1, "e": "Transcription in the nucleus."}]}, {"t": "II.2 Cell growth and division", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>relate chromosomes, DNA, genes and alleles</li><li>compare mitosis and meiosis</li><li>explain how crossing over and independent assortment cause variation</li><li>describe the roles of mitosis and meiosis in life cycles</li></ul></div><h3>1. Chromosomes, genes and alleles</h3><p>Chromosome = a long DNA molecule with many genes. Genes come in versions called <b>alleles</b> (e.g. tall/short). Body cells are <b>diploid (2n)</b> with pairs of homologous chromosomes (humans 46); gametes are <b>haploid (n)</b> (23).</p><h3>2. Mitosis vs meiosis</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 120\" font-size=\"10\"><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"30\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#c9b6ef\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"34\">2n</text><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"85\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#c9b6ef\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"85\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#c9b6ef\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"89\">2n</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"89\">2n</text><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"72\" stroke=\"#555\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"72\" stroke=\"#555\"/><text x=\"15\" y=\"115\">MITOSIS</text><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#fff3cd\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"24\">2n</text><circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"95\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#fde2e2\"/><text x=\"165\" y=\"99\">n</text><circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"95\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#fde2e2\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"99\">n</text><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"95\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#fde2e2\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"99\">n</text><circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"95\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#fde2e2\"/><text x=\"285\" y=\"99\">n</text><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"58\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#fff3cd\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"58\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#fff3cd\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"117\">MEIOSIS: 4 different haploid cells</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Mitosis: 2 identical diploid cells. Meiosis: 4 genetically different haploid cells.</div></div><table><tr><th></th><th>Mitosis</th><th>Meiosis</th></tr><tr><td>Divisions</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Daughter cells</td><td>2, identical</td><td>4, all different</td></tr><tr><td>Chromosome number</td><td>same (2n)</td><td>halved (n)</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>growth, repair, asexual reproduction</td><td>making gametes / spores</td></tr></table><h3>3. Sources of variation</h3><ul><li><b>Crossing over</b>: homologous chromosomes swap sections → new combinations of alleles (recombination).</li><li><b>Independent assortment</b>: each pair lines up randomly, so gametes get different mixes.</li><li>Random fertilisation and mutation add more variation.</li></ul>", "quiz": [{"q": "Meiosis produces…", "o": ["2 identical cells", "4 haploid, genetically different cells", "4 diploid cells", "1 cell"], "a": 1, "e": "For gametes."}, {"q": "Siblings look different mainly because of…", "o": ["mitosis", "binary fission", "DNA replication", "independent assortment in meiosis"], "a": 3, "e": "Plus crossing over and random fertilisation."}, {"q": "A cell has 46 chromosomes. A gamete has…", "o": ["46", "23", "92", "12"], "a": 1, "e": "Haploid = half."}, {"q": "Mitosis is used for…", "o": ["making gametes", "growth and repair", "crossing over", "halving chromosomes"], "a": 1, "e": "Identical cells."}]}, {"t": "II.3 Mendelian inheritance", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>use phenotype, genotype, dominant, recessive, homozygous, heterozygous, co-dominance, incomplete dominance, multiple alleles</li><li>explain ABO blood groups</li><li>solve monohybrid and dihybrid Punnett squares; use a test cross</li></ul></div><h3>1. Key words</h3><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Genotype</td><td>the alleles (e.g. Tt)</td></tr><tr><td>Phenotype</td><td>the visible trait (tall)</td></tr><tr><td>Dominant</td><td>shows when present (T)</td></tr><tr><td>Recessive</td><td>shows only when homozygous (tt)</td></tr><tr><td>Homozygous / heterozygous</td><td>same alleles (TT, tt) / different (Tt)</td></tr><tr><td>Co-dominance</td><td>both show (roan cattle: red + white hairs; blood group AB)</td></tr><tr><td>Incomplete dominance</td><td>a blend (red × white → pink flowers)</td></tr></table><h3>2. Punnett squares</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>T</th><th>t</th></tr><tr><td><b>T</b></td><td>TT</td><td>Tt</td></tr><tr><td><b>t</b></td><td>Tt</td><td>tt</td></tr></table><p>Tt × Tt → genotypes 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt; phenotypes <b>3 tall : 1 short</b>.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Dihybrid RrYy × RrYy → 9 : 3 : 3 : 1. Ssww × ssWw → 1 : 1 : 1 : 1 (KSSC 2024).</div><p><b>Test cross</b>: cross the unknown (dominant phenotype) with a homozygous recessive. All dominant offspring → unknown was homozygous; 1 : 1 → heterozygous.</p><h3>3. Blood groups (multiple alleles)</h3><p>Alleles Iᴬ, Iᴮ (co-dominant) and i (recessive). A = IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi; B = IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi; AB = IᴬIᴮ; O = ii. An A (Iᴬi) and B (Iᴮi) parent can have children of all four groups.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Heterozygous means…", "o": ["two identical alleles", "two different alleles for a gene", "no alleles", "two genes"], "a": 1, "e": "E.g. Rr."}, {"q": "Rr × rr. The proportion of offspring with the recessive phenotype is…", "o": ["0%", "25%", "50%", "100%"], "a": 2, "e": "Rr, Rr, rr, rr."}, {"q": "Rr × Rr gives a phenotype ratio of…", "o": ["1:1", "3:1", "1:2:1", "all dominant"], "a": 1, "e": "3 dominant : 1 recessive."}, {"q": "RrYy × RRyy. Phenotypic ratio?", "o": ["9:3:3:1", "3:1", "1 round yellow : 1 round green", "all round yellow"], "a": 2, "e": "All round (R from parent 2); Yy or yy in equal numbers."}, {"q": "The physical appearance of an organism is its…", "o": ["genotype", "phenotype", "allele", "gene pool"], "a": 1, "e": "Phenotype."}]}, {"t": "II.4 Applications of genetics", "w": 3, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe selective breeding of plants and animals</li><li>discuss advantages and disadvantages of genetic modification</li></ul></div><h3>1. Selective breeding</h3><p>Humans choose parents with useful traits and breed them over many generations: high-yield rice, disease-resistant taro, pigs that grow fast, chickens that lay more eggs, cows that give more milk. It reduces genetic variety (more risk from disease).</p><h3>2. Genetic modification (GM)</h3><p>A gene from one organism is inserted into another (e.g. the human insulin gene into bacteria, which then make insulin for diabetics).</p><table><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr><tr><td>disease/pest-resistant crops; higher yields</td><td>genes may spread to wild plants</td></tr><tr><td>cheap medicines (insulin, vaccines)</td><td>unknown long-term health effects</td></tr><tr><td>bacteria to clean oil spills</td><td>expensive seeds owned by companies; ethical concerns</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Producing insulin in bacteria is an example of…", "o": ["selective breeding", "genetic modification", "natural selection", "cloning by cuttings"], "a": 1, "e": "A human gene is inserted."}, {"q": "Selective breeding means…", "o": ["random mating", "humans choosing parents with wanted traits", "changing DNA in a lab", "mutation"], "a": 1, "e": "Artificial selection."}, {"q": "A risk of selective breeding is…", "o": ["too much variation", "loss of genetic variation", "faster evolution", "no effect"], "a": 1, "e": "Inbreeding reduces variety."}, {"q": "A benefit of GM crops is…", "o": ["always cheaper seeds", "resistance to pests", "no testing needed", "more weeds"], "a": 1, "e": "e.g. Bt maize."}]}, {"t": "III.1 Plant transport", "w": 5, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain why plants need transport tissues</li><li>describe xylem, phloem and cambium in monocot and dicot stems</li><li>relate root hair structure to function</li><li>explain transpiration and the factors affecting it; stomata and guard cells; factors affecting photosynthesis</li></ul></div><h3>1. Xylem and phloem</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 130\" font-size=\"10\"><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"65\" r=\"55\" fill=\"#e6f4e2\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"108.0\" cy=\"65.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"96.87005768508881\" cy=\"91.8700576850888\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"103.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"43.1299423149112\" cy=\"91.87005768508881\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"32.0\" cy=\"65.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"43.12994231491119\" cy=\"38.1299423149112\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"27.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"96.8700576850888\" cy=\"38.12994231491119\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"128\">DICOT: bundles in a ring</text><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"65\" r=\"55\" fill=\"#e6f4e2\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"45\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"30\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"255\" cy=\"70\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"75\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"90\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"245\" cy=\"100\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"265\" cy=\"40\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"55\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"25\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#8a6d1c\"/><text x=\"195\" y=\"128\">MONOCOT: scattered</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Vascular bundles in stem cross-sections (xylem on the inside, phloem outside)</div></div><table><tr><th>Xylem</th><th>Phloem</th></tr><tr><td>water and minerals</td><td>sugars (sucrose) and amino acids</td></tr><tr><td>roots → leaves (one way)</td><td>leaves → all parts (both ways): translocation</td></tr><tr><td>dead, hollow, lignified tubes</td><td>living sieve tubes + companion cells</td></tr></table><p><b>Cambium</b> (dicots) divides to make new xylem and phloem. Root hairs give a huge surface area; water enters by osmosis, minerals by active transport.</p><h3>2. Transpiration and stomata</h3><p><b>Transpiration</b> = loss of water vapour from leaves, mainly through <b>stomata</b>. It pulls water up the xylem (transpiration stream), cools the leaf and carries minerals.</p><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Faster transpiration when…</th></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>hotter</td></tr><tr><td>Wind</td><td>windier (removes humid air)</td></tr><tr><td>Humidity</td><td>drier air</td></tr><tr><td>Light</td><td>brighter (stomata open)</td></tr><tr><td>Water availability</td><td>plenty of soil water</td></tr></table><p><b>Guard cells</b> take in K⁺ and water, swell and curve apart → the stoma opens (day); losing water closes it (night or drought).</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Xylem transports…", "o": ["sugar from leaf to root", "water and minerals", "oxygen", "hormones only"], "a": 1, "e": "Upwards from the roots."}, {"q": "Phloem transports…", "o": ["water", "sugars from the leaves", "minerals only", "CO₂"], "a": 1, "e": "Translocation."}, {"q": "Guard cells open stomata by…", "o": ["losing water", "taking in K⁺ and water", "dying", "making starch"], "a": 1, "e": "They become turgid."}, {"q": "Root hair cells absorb minerals by…", "o": ["osmosis", "diffusion only", "active transport", "evaporation"], "a": 2, "e": "Against the concentration gradient."}, {"q": "Transpiration is fastest on a day that is…", "o": ["cold, still and humid", "hot, windy and dry", "dark", "rainy"], "a": 1, "e": "All factors increase evaporation."}]}, {"t": "III.2 Plant life cycles – alternation of generations", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>compare sporophyte and gametophyte in ferns and angiosperms (mitosis, meiosis, n/2n, fertilisation, dispersal)</li><li>compare sexual and asexual reproduction in plants</li></ul></div><h3>1. Alternation of generations</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 150\" font-size=\"11\"><ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"75\" rx=\"120\" ry=\"58\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><text x=\"125\" y=\"14\">SPOROPHYTE (2n)</text><text x=\"265\" y=\"80\">meiosis → spores (n)</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"145\">GAMETOPHYTE (n)</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"80\">zygote (2n) ←</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"93\">fertilisation</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"128\">mitosis → gametes</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#c0392b\">2n</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#c0392b\">2n</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">n</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">n</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Plants alternate between a diploid sporophyte and a haploid gametophyte</div></div><table><tr><th></th><th>Fern</th><th>Flowering plant (angiosperm)</th></tr><tr><td>Main plant</td><td>sporophyte (fronds)</td><td>sporophyte (the whole plant)</td></tr><tr><td>Gametophyte</td><td>small, free-living prothallus</td><td>tiny: pollen grain (♂), embryo sac (♀)</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilisation</td><td>needs <b>water</b> for sperm to swim</td><td>pollen tube, no water needed</td></tr><tr><td>Dispersal</td><td>spores</td><td>seeds and fruits</td></tr></table><h3>2. Sexual vs asexual reproduction</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>Sexual</th><th>Asexual</th></tr><tr><td>Parents</td><td>two (or gametes from one)</td><td>one</td></tr><tr><td>Offspring</td><td>varied</td><td>identical clones</td></tr><tr><td>Advantage</td><td>variation → survive change</td><td>fast, no pollinator needed, good traits kept</td></tr><tr><td>Disadvantage</td><td>slower, needs pollination</td><td>no variation, disease can wipe out all</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>seeds of breadfruit, pawpaw</td><td>taro suckers, banana suckers, cuttings, runners</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "In a flowering plant's life cycle, the gametophyte…", "o": ["is diploid", "forms gametes by mitosis", "forms spores by meiosis", "is the large plant"], "a": 1, "e": "The sporophyte forms spores by meiosis."}, {"q": "Spores are produced by…", "o": ["mitosis in the gametophyte", "meiosis in the sporophyte", "fertilisation", "pollination"], "a": 1, "e": "Meiosis."}, {"q": "Ferns need water for…", "o": ["photosynthesis only", "sperm to swim to the egg", "spore dispersal", "germination of seeds"], "a": 1, "e": "Fertilisation depends on water."}, {"q": "Banana suckers are an example of…", "o": ["sexual reproduction", "asexual reproduction", "pollination", "fertilisation"], "a": 1, "e": "Vegetative propagation."}]}, {"t": "III.3 Human nutrition and health", "w": 5, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain the effect of modern diets (fat, sugar) on Pacific Islanders</li><li>explain effects of low-fibre diet, gastric ulcers, gall stones</li><li>explain the liver's role in blood sugar and diabetes</li></ul></div><h3>1. Traditional vs modern diets</h3><table><tr><th>Traditional</th><th>Modern (imported, processed)</th></tr><tr><td>fish, coconut, babai, breadfruit, pandanus, local fruit</td><td>rice, flour, sugar, tinned meat, noodles, soft drinks</td></tr><tr><td>high fibre, vitamins</td><td>high fat, sugar, salt; low fibre</td></tr><tr><td>active lifestyle</td><td>less physical activity</td></tr></table><p>The change has led to high rates of <b>obesity, type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure and heart disease (NCDs)</b> in Kiribati and the Pacific.</p><h3>2. Digestive disorders</h3><ul><li><b>Low-fibre diet</b>: food moves slowly → <b>constipation</b>; linked to bowel cancer. Fibre helps peristalsis.</li><li><b>Gastric ulcers</b>: acid damages the stomach lining (bacteria H. pylori, aspirin, alcohol, stress) → heartburn, bleeding, possibly cancer.</li><li><b>Gall stones</b>: hardened cholesterol in the gall bladder block bile release → poor fat digestion and pain.</li></ul><h3>3. Blood sugar control and diabetes</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 120\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"115\" y=\"50\" width=\"100\" height=\"24\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dff0d8\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\"/><text x=\"122\" y=\"66\">normal glucose</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"25\">HIGH (after a meal)</text><text x=\"5\" y=\"40\">→ insulin → liver stores glycogen</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"25\">LOW (exercise, fasting)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"40\">→ glucagon → liver</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"53\">releases glucose</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"105\">negative feedback keeps blood glucose steady</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The pancreas and liver control blood glucose</div></div><p><b>Type 1 diabetes</b>: the pancreas makes no insulin (needs injections). <b>Type 2</b>: body cells resist insulin, linked to obesity and poor diet; controlled by diet, exercise, medicine. Symptoms: thirst, frequent urination, tiredness; complications: blindness, amputations, kidney failure.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Fibre is important because it…", "o": ["neutralises acid", "increases protein absorption", "gives extra energy", "stimulates peristalsis and prevents constipation"], "a": 3, "e": "It adds bulk."}, {"q": "Insulin causes the liver to…", "o": ["release glucose", "store glucose as glycogen", "make glucagon", "make bile"], "a": 1, "e": "It lowers blood glucose."}, {"q": "Type 2 diabetes is mainly caused by…", "o": ["no pancreas", "cells resisting insulin", "too much glucagon", "infection"], "a": 1, "e": "Linked to lifestyle."}, {"q": "A diet high in sugar and fat increases the risk of…", "o": ["scurvy", "type 2 diabetes", "rickets", "anaemia only"], "a": 1, "e": "NCDs."}]}, {"t": "III.4 Animal digestion", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation, egestion</li><li>compare sac gut, tube gut and complex gut with habitat and way of life</li></ul></div><h3>1. The five stages</h3><table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Ingestion</td><td>taking food into the mouth</td></tr><tr><td>Digestion</td><td>breaking large insoluble molecules into small soluble ones (mechanical + chemical/enzymes)</td></tr><tr><td>Absorption</td><td>small molecules pass into the blood (villi of the small intestine)</td></tr><tr><td>Assimilation</td><td>body uses them to build cells and release energy</td></tr><tr><td>Egestion</td><td>undigested food (faeces) removed through the anus</td></tr></table><h3>2. Types of gut</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 110\" font-size=\"10\"><path d=\"M30 20 L30 80 Q50 100 70 80 L70 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"105\">SAC (sea anemone): 1 opening</text><path d=\"M130 50 L220 50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"12\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"125\" y=\"35\">mouth → → anus</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"80\">TUBE (earthworm)</text><path d=\"M250 20 Q320 30 270 50 Q230 70 300 80 L300 95\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"108\">COMPLEX (human)</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Sac gut, tube gut and complex gut</div></div><table><tr><th>Gut</th><th>Openings</th><th>Features</th><th>Way of life</th></tr><tr><td>Sac</td><td>1 (mouth = anus)</td><td>food and waste through one hole; no specialised regions</td><td>sessile (stays still), catches occasional prey with tentacles</td></tr><tr><td>Tube</td><td>2</td><td>one-way flow; crop, gizzard, intestine</td><td>slow-moving, eats soil continuously</td></tr><tr><td>Complex</td><td>2</td><td>many specialised regions and enzymes, villi, long gut</td><td>active, varied diet, needs lots of energy</td></tr></table><p>More complex guts are <b>more efficient</b>: continuous feeding, specialised digestion and absorption.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Ingestion takes place in the…", "o": ["stomach", "mouth", "small intestine", "anus"], "a": 1, "e": "Taking in food."}, {"q": "Absorption mainly happens in the…", "o": ["stomach", "small intestine", "large intestine", "mouth"], "a": 1, "e": "Villi."}, {"q": "A sea anemone has a…", "o": ["tube gut", "sac gut", "complex gut", "no gut"], "a": 1, "e": "One opening."}, {"q": "Removing undigested food is called…", "o": ["excretion", "egestion", "assimilation", "ingestion"], "a": 1, "e": "Egestion ≠ excretion."}]}, {"t": "III.5 Gas exchange in animals", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>distinguish breathing, gas exchange and cellular respiration</li><li>compare body surface, gills, tracheae and lungs with size and way of life</li></ul></div><h3>1. Three different processes</h3><table><tr><th>Breathing (ventilation)</th><th>Gas exchange</th><th>Cellular respiration</th></tr><tr><td>muscle movements moving air in and out</td><td>diffusion of O₂ in and CO₂ out across a surface</td><td>chemical reaction in cells making ATP</td></tr></table><p>A good gas exchange surface is <b>large, thin, moist</b>, with a <b>blood supply</b> and ventilation to keep a concentration gradient.</p><h3>2. Comparing systems</h3><table><tr><th>System</th><th>Example</th><th>How it works</th><th>Suits</th></tr><tr><td>Body surface</td><td>earthworm</td><td>O₂ diffuses through moist skin to blood</td><td>small, slow, damp places</td></tr><tr><td>Gills</td><td>fish, crab</td><td>water over thin filaments; <b>countercurrent</b> flow keeps the gradient</td><td>water (low O₂)</td></tr><tr><td>Tracheae</td><td>cockroach</td><td>air tubes from spiracles straight to cells</td><td>small insects on land</td></tr><tr><td>Lungs</td><td>human</td><td>alveoli: huge area, one cell thick, many capillaries</td><td>large, active, land</td></tr></table><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 70\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"15\" width=\"280\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#cfe3fb\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"35\" width=\"280\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#f8c8c8\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"26\">water → → → (O₂ high→low)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"46\">← ← ← blood (O₂ low)</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"65\">countercurrent: blood always meets water with more O₂</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Countercurrent exchange in fish gills</div></div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Correct sequence in the respiratory process", "o": ["Gas exchange → transport → inhalation → respiration", "Inhalation → transport → gas exchange → respiration", "Transport → inhalation → gas exchange → respiration", "Inhalation → gas exchange → transport → respiration"], "a": 3, "e": "Air in, exchange at the alveoli, carried by blood, used in cells."}, {"q": "Insects exchange gases through…", "o": ["gills", "lungs", "the tracheal system", "the skin only"], "a": 2, "e": "Spiracles and tracheae."}, {"q": "Countercurrent flow in fish gills…", "o": ["reduces oxygen uptake", "keeps a concentration gradient along the whole gill", "stops blood flow", "is found in insects"], "a": 1, "e": "Water and blood flow in opposite directions."}, {"q": "Alveoli are efficient because they are…", "o": ["thick and dry", "thin, moist, with a large surface area", "few and large", "without blood"], "a": 1, "e": "Short diffusion distance."}]}, {"t": "III.6 Animal circulatory systems", "w": 5, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain why large animals need a transport system</li><li>compare open, closed single and closed double circulation</li><li>explain how smoking, alcohol and obesity cause coronary heart disease</li></ul></div><h3>1. Why a transport system?</h3><p>Large animals have a small surface area to volume ratio; diffusion alone is too slow to reach inner cells, so blood carries O₂, food and wastes.</p><h3>2. Types of circulation</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 125\" font-size=\"10\"><text x=\"10\" y=\"10\">SINGLE (fish)</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"30\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"15\" y=\"85\">heart</text><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"88\" y=\"31\">gills</text><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"95\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"87\" y=\"98\">body</text><path d=\"M50 55 Q75 30 75 28 M125 28 Q150 60 125 95 M75 95 Q35 95 35 70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"185\" y=\"10\">DOUBLE (human)</text><rect x=\"215\" y=\"52\" width=\"40\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"218\" y=\"88\">heart</text><ellipse cx=\"235\" cy=\"25\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"222\" y=\"28\">lungs</text><ellipse cx=\"235\" cy=\"108\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"223\" y=\"111\">body</text><path d=\"M222 52 Q195 35 210 25 M260 25 Q275 35 250 52 M222 74 Q195 95 210 108 M260 108 Q280 95 250 74\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"60\">through the</text><text x=\"268\" y=\"72\">heart twice</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Single (fish) and double (mammal) circulation</div></div><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Features</th></tr><tr><td>Open</td><td>insects</td><td>blood pumped into a body cavity, bathes organs, low pressure, slow</td></tr><tr><td>Closed single</td><td>fish</td><td>heart → gills → body → heart; pressure drops after the gills</td></tr><tr><td>Closed double</td><td>humans</td><td>heart → lungs → heart → body; high pressure, fast, efficient for active warm animals</td></tr></table><h3>3. Coronary heart disease</h3><p>Fatty deposits (cholesterol <b>plaque</b>) build up in the <b>coronary arteries</b>, which narrows them so less O₂ reaches the heart muscle → angina or a heart attack.</p><ul><li><b>Smoking</b>: nicotine raises blood pressure; CO reduces O₂ carried; damages artery walls.</li><li><b>Alcohol</b>: raises blood pressure and fats in the blood.</li><li><b>Obesity</b>: high-fat diet raises cholesterol; extra weight strains the heart.</li></ul>", "quiz": [{"q": "A common effect of smoking, obesity and alcohol on the heart", "o": ["better oxygen transport", "lower risk of heart disease", "more elastic vessels", "arteries narrowed by plaque"], "a": 3, "e": "Atherosclerosis."}, {"q": "Which animals have an open circulatory system?", "o": ["humans", "fish", "insects", "earthworms"], "a": 2, "e": "Haemolymph bathes the organs."}, {"q": "In a double circulation, blood goes through the heart…", "o": ["once per circuit", "twice per circuit", "never", "three times"], "a": 1, "e": "Lungs and body loops."}, {"q": "Why do large animals need a circulatory system?", "o": ["high SA:V ratio", "low SA:V ratio, diffusion too slow", "they are cold", "they don't respire"], "a": 1, "e": "Diffusion distance too large."}]}, {"t": "III.7 Excretion in animals", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain the production of CO₂, water and nitrogenous wastes</li><li>compare ammonia, urea and uric acid with environment</li><li>explain kidney stones and gout</li></ul></div><h3>1. Wastes and where they come from</h3><p>Respiration makes CO₂ and water; breaking down excess <b>amino acids</b> (deamination in the liver) makes nitrogenous waste.</p><table><tr><th>Waste</th><th>Animal</th><th>Toxicity</th><th>Water needed</th></tr><tr><td>Ammonia</td><td>fish</td><td>very toxic</td><td>a lot (diluted in water)</td></tr><tr><td>Urea</td><td>humans, mammals</td><td>less toxic</td><td>moderate</td></tr><tr><td>Uric acid</td><td>insects, birds, reptiles</td><td>least toxic, a paste</td><td>very little (saves water on land)</td></tr></table><h3>2. The kidney</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 110\" font-size=\"10\"><path d=\"M40 30 Q20 10 40 10 Q60 10 50 30 L50 70 Q60 90 90 90 L250 90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"15\">glomerulus: FILTRATION</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"55\">tubule: REABSORPTION of glucose,</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"67\">water and salts</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"105\">→ urine to bladder</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A nephron: filter, then take back what is useful</div></div><p>Glucose is reabsorbed by active transport; urea passes out in urine. Drinking more water → more, dilute urine; sweating → less, concentrated urine.</p><h3>3. Kidney stones and gout</h3><p><b>Kidney stones</b>: salts (calcium oxalate, uric acid) crystallise in the kidney when urine is concentrated (not drinking enough water) → severe pain, blocked urine flow.</p><p><b>Gout</b>: too much <b>uric acid</b> in the blood (diet rich in red meat, seafood, alcohol) forms sharp crystals in joints (big toe) → painful, swollen joints.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Glucose is reabsorbed in the kidney by…", "o": ["filtration", "active transport", "osmosis only", "excretion"], "a": 1, "e": "Selective reabsorption uses ATP."}, {"q": "Fish excrete mainly…", "o": ["urea", "uric acid", "ammonia", "glucose"], "a": 2, "e": "Lots of water available."}, {"q": "Gout is caused by crystals of…", "o": ["urea", "uric acid", "calcium", "glucose"], "a": 1, "e": "Uric acid in joints."}, {"q": "Birds excrete uric acid because it…", "o": ["needs little water", "is very toxic", "is a gas", "makes feathers"], "a": 0, "e": "Saves water."}]}, {"t": "III.8 Reproduction in mammals", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain oestrogen and progesterone in the menstrual cycle</li><li>describe events leading to implantation</li><li>explain the role of the placenta</li></ul></div><h3>1. The menstrual cycle</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 140\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><path d=\"M40 100 Q90 100 120 40 Q140 90 300 95\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"132\">day 0 → 28</text><text x=\"2\" y=\"12\">hormone</text><path d=\"M40 105 Q150 105 180 55 Q230 40 260 100 L290 105\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\">oestrogen</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"50\" fill=\"#c0392b\">progesterone</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"12\">ovulation ~day 14</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Hormone levels in a 28-day cycle</div></div><table><tr><th>Days</th><th>Event</th></tr><tr><td>1–5</td><td>menstruation (lining shed)</td></tr><tr><td>5–14</td><td><b>oestrogen</b> rises: lining rebuilds, egg matures</td></tr><tr><td>~14</td><td>ovulation: egg released</td></tr><tr><td>14–28</td><td><b>progesterone</b> (from the corpus luteum) keeps the lining thick; if no pregnancy, it falls and menstruation starts</td></tr></table><h3>2. Fertilisation and implantation</h3><p>Sperm meets the egg in the <b>oviduct</b> → zygote divides into a ball of cells (embryo) as it moves down → <b>implants</b> in the thick uterus lining about a week later. Progesterone keeps the lining in place.</p><h3>3. The placenta</h3><p>Mother's and baby's blood come very close but <b>do not mix</b>. Across the placenta:</p><ul><li>O₂, glucose, amino acids, <b>antibodies</b> → to the foetus</li><li>CO₂, urea ← from the foetus</li><li>harmful drugs, alcohol, nicotine and some viruses can also cross</li></ul><p>The <b>umbilical vein</b> carries oxygenated blood to the foetus; the umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood back.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Implantation is when…", "o": ["the egg is released", "the embryo attaches to the uterus lining", "sperm meets egg", "the baby is born"], "a": 1, "e": "Progesterone maintains the lining."}, {"q": "The hormone that rebuilds the uterus lining", "o": ["progesterone", "oestrogen", "insulin", "testosterone"], "a": 1, "e": "Oestrogen."}, {"q": "Which can harm the foetus by crossing the placenta?", "o": ["antibodies", "alcohol", "oxygen", "glucose"], "a": 1, "e": "Alcohol."}, {"q": "Ovulation usually happens around day…", "o": ["1", "7", "14", "28"], "a": 2, "e": "Mid-cycle."}]}, {"t": "IV.1 Diversity of organisms and classification", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>classify organisms into the main groups and give examples</li><li>use a dichotomous key</li></ul></div><h3>1. Main groups</h3><table><tr><th>Group</th><th>Features</th><th>Pacific example</th></tr><tr><td>Bacteria</td><td>single cells, no nucleus</td><td>cholera, nitrogen-fixing bacteria</td></tr><tr><td>Algae</td><td>simple plants, no roots</td><td>seaweed, phytoplankton</td></tr><tr><td>Fungi</td><td>hyphae, spores, feed on dead matter</td><td>mushrooms, bread mould</td></tr><tr><td>Ferns</td><td>spores, fronds</td><td>tree fern</td></tr><tr><td>Angiosperms</td><td>flowers, seeds in fruits; monocots and dicots</td><td>coconut (monocot), breadfruit (dicot)</td></tr><tr><td>Coelenterates</td><td>tentacles, sac gut</td><td>jellyfish, coral, sea anemone</td></tr><tr><td>Annelids</td><td>segmented worms</td><td>earthworm</td></tr><tr><td>Molluscs</td><td>soft body, often a shell</td><td>clams, octopus</td></tr><tr><td>Arthropods</td><td>jointed legs, exoskeleton</td><td>insects, crabs, spiders, centipedes</td></tr><tr><td>Echinoderms</td><td>spiny skin, 5-part symmetry</td><td>sea urchin, starfish, sea cucumber</td></tr><tr><td>Chordates</td><td>backbone</td><td>fish, frogs, reptiles, birds, mammals</td></tr></table><p>Naming: <b>binomial</b> system: <i>Genus species</i>, e.g. <i>Carcharodon carcharias</i> (great white shark).</p><h3>2. Dichotomous keys</h3><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Example key</b>1a Has legs ........ go to 2<br>1b No legs ........ earthworm<br>2a Six legs ........ insect<br>2b More than six legs ........ go to 3<br>3a Eight legs ........ spider<br>3b Many legs ........ centipede</div><p>Each step splits into two choices using one clear feature you can see.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Coral belongs to the…", "o": ["molluscs", "coelenterates", "echinoderms", "arthropods"], "a": 1, "e": "Tentacles and sac gut."}, {"q": "Crabs are…", "o": ["molluscs", "arthropods", "annelids", "chordates"], "a": 1, "e": "Jointed legs."}, {"q": "In Homo sapiens, \"Homo\" is the…", "o": ["species", "genus", "family", "class"], "a": 1, "e": "First name = genus."}, {"q": "A coconut palm is a…", "o": ["dicot", "monocot", "fern", "gymnosperm"], "a": 1, "e": "Parallel veins, one seed leaf."}]}, {"t": "IV.2 Adaptive features", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define adaptation; classify as structural, physiological, behavioural or life-history</li><li>relate adaptations to tolerance, niche, Gause's principle and Liebig's law</li></ul></div><h3>1. Types of adaptation</h3><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Structural</td><td>body shape or parts</td><td>streamlined shark; thick waxy leaves of pandanus</td></tr><tr><td>Physiological</td><td>body chemistry/function</td><td>fish gills removing salt; mangrove salt glands</td></tr><tr><td>Behavioural</td><td>what it does</td><td>crabs hide in burrows at low tide; turtles nest at night</td></tr><tr><td>Life history</td><td>timing of the life cycle</td><td>coral spawning on certain full moons</td></tr></table><h3>2. Ecological ideas</h3><ul><li><b>Tolerance range</b>: the range of a factor (temperature, salinity) an organism can survive; best in the optimum zone.</li><li><b>Niche</b>: an organism's role and way of life in its habitat (what it eats, where, when).</li><li><b>Gause's principle</b> (competitive exclusion): two species with exactly the same niche cannot coexist – one outcompetes the other.</li><li><b>Liebig's law of the minimum</b>: growth is limited by the scarcest resource (the limiting factor).</li></ul>", "quiz": [{"q": "Gause's principle states…", "o": ["competition has no effect", "two species share a niche forever", "species always avoid competition", "one species outcompetes the other for the same limited resource"], "a": 3, "e": "Competitive exclusion."}, {"q": "A turtle laying eggs at night is a…", "o": ["structural", "physiological", "behavioural", "life-history"], "a": 2, "e": "Behaviour."}, {"q": "Liebig's law says growth is limited by…", "o": ["the most plentiful resource", "the scarcest resource", "temperature only", "predators"], "a": 1, "e": "Limiting factor."}, {"q": "An organism's role and way of life is its…", "o": ["habitat", "niche", "population", "community"], "a": 1, "e": "Niche."}]}, {"t": "IV.3 Populations", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe size (number, natality, mortality) and space (territory, home range, distribution, density)</li><li>explain survivorship curves</li><li>explain the value of genetic variation and effects of isolation</li><li>estimate populations with quadrats, transects and mark–recapture</li></ul></div><h3>1. Characteristics</h3><p><b>Population</b> = all members of one species in an area. Size changes with natality (births), mortality (deaths), immigration and emigration. Density = number ÷ area. Distribution can be clumped, random or uniform.</p><h3>2. Survivorship curves</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 140\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><path d=\"M40 15 L200 20 Q240 30 260 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"132\">age</text><text x=\"2\" y=\"12\">survivors (log)</text><path d=\"M40 15 L260 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M40 15 Q50 105 260 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"22\">I humans</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"65\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">II birds</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#c0392b\">III oysters, fish</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Type I: most live to old age. Type III: most die young (many eggs, little care).</div></div><h3>3. Variation and isolation</h3><p>Genetic variation means some individuals survive a disease or change, so the population survives. A small <b>isolated</b> population (e.g. on one atoll) loses variation, risks inbreeding, and over time may become a new species.</p><h3>4. Estimating populations</h3><div class=\"key\">Mark–recapture: N = (M × C) ÷ R</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Mark 40 crabs; next day catch 50, of which 10 are marked. N = 40 × 50/10 = <b>200 crabs</b>.</div><p>Quadrats (squares) for plants and slow animals; transects (lines) to show changes across a beach or reef.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Mark–recapture is used to…", "o": ["measure pH", "estimate population size", "count chromosomes", "measure biomass"], "a": 1, "e": "N = (M × C)/R."}, {"q": "A Type III survivorship organism", "o": ["human", "elephant", "oyster", "bird"], "a": 2, "e": "Many offspring, high early mortality."}, {"q": "Natality means…", "o": ["death rate", "birth rate", "migration", "density"], "a": 1, "e": "Births."}, {"q": "Mark 30, recapture 60 with 12 marked. N =", "o": ["150", "90", "360", "24"], "a": 0, "e": "30 × 60/12."}]}, {"t": "IV.4 Communities", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe species composition, zonation, stratification, succession and climax</li><li>describe pioneer species</li><li>explain competition; describe predation, parasitism, mutualism, commensalism</li></ul></div><h3>1. Community patterns</h3><p>A <b>community</b> = all the populations living together. <b>Zonation</b>: bands of species across a gradient (rocky shore, mangroves → coconut zone). <b>Stratification</b>: vertical layers (forest canopy, shrubs, floor). <b>Succession</b>: gradual change from <b>pioneer species</b> (lichens, grasses, beach plants on new sand) to a stable <b>climax community</b>.</p><h3>2. Relationships</h3><table><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Effect</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Predation</td><td>+ / −</td><td>shark eats fish</td></tr><tr><td>Parasitism</td><td>+ / −</td><td>tapeworm in a pig; lice</td></tr><tr><td>Mutualism</td><td>+ / +</td><td>coral and zooxanthellae algae; clownfish and anemone</td></tr><tr><td>Commensalism</td><td>+ / 0</td><td>remora on a shark; barnacles on a whale</td></tr><tr><td>Competition</td><td>− / −</td><td>two fish species for the same food</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "Both organisms benefit in…", "o": ["predation", "mutualism", "parasitism", "commensalism"], "a": 1, "e": "+/+"}, {"q": "Bands of vegetation changing with altitude are called…", "o": ["succession", "zonation", "migration", "mutation"], "a": 1, "e": "Zonation."}, {"q": "Coral and the algae living in it show…", "o": ["parasitism", "mutualism", "predation", "competition"], "a": 1, "e": "Both benefit."}, {"q": "The first organisms to colonise bare rock are…", "o": ["climax species", "pioneer species", "predators", "parasites"], "a": 1, "e": "e.g. lichens."}]}, {"t": "IV.5 Ecosystems", "w": 4, "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe biotic and abiotic factors, food chains and webs, energy flow, ecological pyramids, carbon and nitrogen cycles</li><li>relate biodiversity to ecosystem survival</li><li>investigate a local environmental issue</li></ul></div><h3>1. Energy flow</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 120\" font-size=\"11\"><polygon points=\"60,110 260,110 230,85 90,85\" fill=\"#9cd39f\"/><polygon points=\"90,85 230,85 205,60 115,60\" fill=\"#d4e6a5\"/><polygon points=\"115,60 205,60 182,35 138,35\" fill=\"#f3d27a\"/><polygon points=\"138,35 182,35 160,10\" fill=\"#f0a35e\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"102\">producers</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"77\">herbivores</text><text x=\"135\" y=\"52\">carnivores</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"40\">≈10% passed up;</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"55\">90% lost as</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"70\">heat, waste</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Pyramid of energy</div></div><p>Phytoplankton → zooplankton → small fish → tuna → shark. Energy enters by photosynthesis and flows <b>one way</b>; nutrients are <b>recycled</b> by decomposers. Pyramids of numbers can be inverted (one tree, many insects); pyramids of energy never are.</p><h3>2. Nutrient cycles</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 120\" font-size=\"11\"><text x=\"120\" y=\"15\">CO₂ in the air</text><text x=\"5\" y=\"60\">photosynthesis ↓</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"60\">↑ respiration</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"75\">↑ combustion</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"110\">plants → animals → decomposers</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The carbon cycle</div></div><p><b>Nitrogen cycle</b>: nitrogen-fixing bacteria (in legume root nodules) turn N₂ into ammonium; nitrifying bacteria make nitrates; plants absorb nitrates to make proteins; decomposers return ammonia; denitrifying bacteria return N₂.</p><h3>3. Biodiversity and local issues</h3><p>High <b>biodiversity</b> makes an ecosystem more stable: if one species declines, others can fill its role. Local issues: coral bleaching, overfishing, sand mining, rubbish and sewage in the lagoon, rising sea level. For your investigation: state a hypothesis, collect data (quadrats, transects, water tests), analyse and conclude.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "A pyramid of numbers can be inverted because…", "o": ["it ignores organism size", "energy increases", "there are no producers", "it uses biomass"], "a": 0, "e": "One big tree can feed thousands of insects."}, {"q": "Nitrogen fixation is done by…", "o": ["nitrifying bacteria", "bacteria in root nodules", "fungi", "plants directly"], "a": 1, "e": "N₂ → ammonium."}, {"q": "About how much energy passes to the next trophic level?", "o": ["90%", "50%", "10%", "1%"], "a": 2, "e": "Most is lost as heat."}, {"q": "Which is an abiotic factor?", "o": ["predators", "salinity", "disease", "competition"], "a": 1, "e": "Non-living."}]}],
papers:[
{y:2025, src:'P2025/KSSC Biology 2025.pdf', note:'Some questions use diagrams. Open the original paper to see them. ⚠ shows where the official marking scheme is wrong.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Enzymes are specific because:',a:'<b>D</b>: each enzyme binds to a particular substrate (complementary active site).'},
{n:'2.',q:'Which visible feature makes the shark a good predator?',a:'<b>D</b>: large sharp teeth (for catching and tearing prey).'},
{n:'3.',q:'What is produced by anaerobic respiration in animals?',a:'<b>A</b>: lactic acid.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Word equation for aerobic respiration',a:'<b>C</b>: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Cell organelles I–IV correctly matched (diagram)',a:'<b>B</b>: chloroplast, mitochondria, cell wall, nucleus (per the marking scheme; check the diagram).'},
{n:'6.',q:'Which is not present in DNA?',a:'<b>A</b>: uracil (it is found in RNA).'},
{n:'7.',q:'Relationship between DNA, gene and chromosome',a:'<b>B</b>: a chromosome contains hundreds of genes, which are made of DNA.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Sequence of protein synthesis',a:'<b>D</b>: transcription → translation → protein folding.'},
{n:'9.',q:'Both organisms benefit',a:'<b>B</b>: mutualism.'},
{n:'10.',q:'Sequence of the respiratory process',a:'<b>D</b>: inhalation → gas exchange → transport of gases in blood → cellular respiration.'},
{n:'11.',q:'Role of NADP⁺ in the light reaction',a:'<b>D</b>: it is reduced to form NADPH.'},
{n:'12.',q:'Why siblings look different',a:'<b>D</b>: independent assortment during meiosis.'},
{n:'13.',q:'Dicot stem: labels III, IV, V',a:'<b>B</b>: ground tissue, vascular bundle, sclerenchyma (per the marking scheme; check the diagram).'},
{n:'14.',q:'Functions of structures I and II',a:'<b>B</b>: I (xylem) transports water and minerals; II (phloem) transports sugar from leaf to root.'},
{n:'15.',q:'Sporophyte and gametophyte in flowering plants',a:'<b>B</b>: the gametophyte forms gametes by mitosis; the sporophyte forms spores by meiosis.'},
{n:'16.',q:'Fibre is important because it:',a:'<b>D</b>: stimulates peristalsis and prevents constipation.'},
{n:'17.',q:'Yeast respiration experiment: gas and new compound after 24 h',a:'<b>A</b>: carbon dioxide and ethanol.'},
{n:'18.',q:'Gause\'s principle',a:'<b>D</b>: when two species compete for the same limited resource, one will eventually outcompete the other (competitive exclusion).', flag:'The marking scheme gives A ("competition has no effect"), which contradicts Gause\'s principle. D is correct.'},
{n:'19.',q:'Common effect of smoking, obesity and alcohol on the heart',a:'<b>D</b>: narrowing of the arteries due to plaque formation.', flag:'The marking scheme gives A ("improved oxygen transport"). That is wrong: these factors damage the heart and blood vessels. D is correct.'},
{n:'20.',q:'Food web: which statement is correct?',a:'<b>A</b>: three producers and three herbivores (per the marking scheme; count the organisms in the diagram to check).'}
]},
{t:'B1 – Cellular biology (28 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1A.',q:'Balanced equation for photosynthesis.',a:'6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ (light energy, chlorophyll)'},
{n:'1B.',q:'(i) Gas X (ii) label the thylakoid (iii) how light-reaction products are used in the Calvin cycle.',a:'(i) <b>Oxygen (O₂)</b>, released from splitting water.\n(ii) The thylakoids are the stacked discs (grana) inside the chloroplast.\n(iii) ATP provides the energy, and NADPH provides electrons/hydrogen (reducing power), to convert CO₂ into G3P (glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate), which is used to make glucose.'},
{n:'2A.',q:'Two roles of oxygen in the electron transport chain.',a:'1. Oxygen is the <b>final electron acceptor</b> at the end of the chain.\n2. It combines with electrons and H⁺ ions to form <b>water</b>. This keeps the electrons flowing and the proton gradient going, so ATP synthase can keep making ATP.'},
{n:'2B.',q:'Compare aerobic and anaerobic respiration: amount of ATP.',a:'Aerobic: about <b>36–38 ATP</b> per glucose (complete breakdown).\nAnaerobic: only <b>2 ATP</b> per glucose (glycolysis only).'},
{n:'2C.',q:'Dominant respiration for (i) a sprinter (ii) a marathon runner.',a:'(i) Sprinter: <b>anaerobic</b> (short, intense effort; the oxygen supply cannot meet the demand).\n(ii) Marathon runner: <b>aerobic</b> (long, steady effort; enough oxygen; much more ATP).'},
{n:'3A.',q:'Kidney: (i) name process 1 (ii) which process requires energy (iii) one substance actively reabsorbed.',a:'(i) <b>Filtration</b> (ultrafiltration in the glomerulus).\n(ii) <b>Reabsorption</b> (selective reabsorption, process 2).\n(iii) <b>Glucose</b> (also amino acids and sodium ions).'},
{n:'3B.',q:'(i) A plant cell in a hypotonic solution (ii) why plant cells don\'t burst but animal cells do.',a:'(i) Water enters by osmosis, the vacuole swells and the cell becomes <b>turgid</b>.\n(ii) Plant cells have a strong cellulose cell wall that resists further expansion. Animal cells have no cell wall, so they keep taking in water and burst (lysis).'},
{n:'3C.',q:'(i) Define facilitated diffusion (ii) one difference between osmosis and diffusion (iii) why root hairs use active transport for minerals.',a:'(i) Passive movement of molecules down their concentration gradient through carrier or channel proteins in the membrane, with no ATP.\n(ii) Osmosis is only the movement of WATER across a partially permeable membrane. Diffusion is the movement of any particles from high to low concentration and needs no membrane.\n(iii) Mineral ions are more concentrated inside the root hair than in the soil, so diffusion would move them OUT. Active transport uses ATP and carrier proteins to move ions IN against the gradient.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Cells 1 and 2: (i) two organelles only in cell 1 (plant) (iii) why both have mitochondria.',a:'(i) <b>Cell wall</b> and <b>chloroplast</b> (also a large permanent vacuole).\n(iii) Both plant and animal cells must respire to release energy (ATP) for living processes, and aerobic respiration happens in the mitochondria.'}
]},
{t:'B2 – Experiment and skills (14 marks)', qs:[
{n:'A.',q:'Describe the induced fit model.',a:'The active site of an enzyme is flexible. When the substrate binds, the active site changes shape slightly to fit around it closely, forming an enzyme–substrate complex. This puts strain on the substrate\'s bonds and lowers the activation energy.'},
{n:'B.',q:'Plot substrate concentration (x) against rate (y).',a:'Points: (0.5, 2.1), (1, 3.9), (2, 6.8), (4, 8.0), (6, 8.2). Label the axes with units, use even scales, and draw a smooth curve that rises steeply and then levels off (plateau about 8.2 mg/min).'},
{n:'C.',q:'Explain the trend.',a:'As substrate concentration increases from 0.5 to 2 g/L, the rate increases steeply (more substrate molecules collide with free active sites). Above about 4 g/L, the rate increases very little and levels off at about 8 mg maltose/min.'},
{n:'D.',q:'Why does the rate level off (induced fit model)?',a:'At high substrate concentration, all the enzyme active sites are occupied (the enzyme is saturated). Each active site must change shape, catalyse and release the product before it can bind another substrate, so adding more substrate cannot increase the rate. The enzyme concentration is now the limiting factor.'},
{n:'E.',q:'One reason the actual rate may be lower than expected at very high substrate concentration.',a:'Product inhibition: a build-up of maltose can block active sites. (Also accepted: substrate inhibition, or the high concentration changing the pH/ionic conditions and slightly altering the enzyme\'s shape.)'}
]},
{t:'B3 – Genetics (21 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define crossing over.',a:'The exchange of sections of DNA between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during prophase I of meiosis, producing new combinations of alleles (genetic variation).'},
{n:'2.',q:'Three differences between mitosis and meiosis.',a:'A. Daughter cells: mitosis → <b>2</b>; meiosis → <b>4</b>.\nB. Chromosome number: mitosis → diploid (2n), the same as the parent; meiosis → haploid (n), half.\nC. Variation: mitosis → genetically identical cells; meiosis → genetically different cells (crossing over, independent assortment).', flag:'The marking scheme table lists 1 and 2 daughter cells. Mitosis gives 2 and meiosis gives 4.'},
{n:'3.',q:'A cell undergoing meiosis has 12 chromosomes. (A) Gametes? (B) Zygote?',a:'A. <b>6</b> chromosomes (haploid).\nB. <b>12</b> chromosomes (6 + 6 at fertilisation).'},
{n:'4.',q:'One benefit and one risk of genetic modification.',a:'Benefit: crops resistant to pests, drought or salt (useful for atoll gardens), giving higher yields and less pesticide use. Or: bacteria engineered to make human insulin.\nRisk: modified genes may spread to wild plants, or harm insects and biodiversity. There may also be unknown long-term health effects.'},
{n:'5A.',q:'Define heterozygous.',a:'Having two different alleles of a gene (e.g. Rr): one dominant and one recessive.'},
{n:'5B.',q:'Genotypes of Plant 1 (heterozygous for both) and Plant 2 (homozygous round, green).',a:'Plant 1: <b>RrYy</b>\nPlant 2: <b>RRyy</b>'},
{n:'5C.',q:'Dihybrid Punnett square.',a:'Plant 1 gametes: RY, Ry, rY, ry. Plant 2 gametes: Ry only.\nRY × Ry → RRYy\nRy × Ry → RRyy\nrY × Ry → RrYy\nry × Ry → Rryy'},
{n:'5D.',q:'Expected phenotypic ratio.',a:'RRYy, RrYy → round yellow; RRyy, Rryy → round green\n<b>1 round yellow : 1 round green</b> (50% : 50%)', flag:'The marking scheme gives ¾ round yellow : ¼ round green. That is wrong: there are 2 yellow (Yy) and 2 green (yy) out of 4, so the ratio is 1:1.'}
]},
{t:'B4 – Organism biology (51 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.1B.',q:'How guard cells control the opening and closing of stomata.',a:'Opening (light): guard cells actively take in K⁺ ions → their water potential falls → water enters by osmosis → they become turgid and bend outwards (the inner wall is thicker) → the stoma opens.\nClosing (dark or water shortage): K⁺ ions leave → water leaves by osmosis → the guard cells become flaccid → the stoma closes, reducing water loss.'},
{n:'1.1C.',q:'What if all stomata stayed closed?',a:'No CO₂ could enter, so photosynthesis would stop and the plant would starve. Transpiration would stop, so water and mineral uptake from the roots would slow and the leaves could overheat. The plant would eventually die.'},
{n:'1.2B.',q:'How is the root hair cell adapted for absorption?',a:'• Long, thin extension → large surface area\n• Thin cell wall and membrane → short diffusion distance\n• Large vacuole with concentrated cell sap → maintains a water potential gradient for osmosis\n• Many mitochondria → ATP for active transport of ions'},
{n:'1.2C.',q:'How does mineral absorption differ from water absorption?',a:'Water enters passively by osmosis (down the water potential gradient, no energy). Minerals are usually more concentrated inside the cell than in the soil, so they must be taken up by active transport, which uses carrier proteins and ATP from respiration.'},
{n:'2A.',q:'Define asexual reproduction and give one example.',a:'Reproduction with only one parent and no gametes; the offspring are genetically identical (clones).\nExample: vegetative propagation, e.g. banana suckers, breadfruit root cuttings or sweet potato vines (also budding in Hydra, binary fission in bacteria).'},
{n:'2B.',q:'One disadvantage of (i) sexual (ii) asexual reproduction.',a:'(i) Sexual: needs two parents (finding a mate or pollinator takes time and energy), and it is slower with fewer offspring.\n(ii) Asexual: no genetic variation, so one disease or environmental change can wipe out the whole population.'},
{n:'3A.',q:'Two ways to prevent gastric ulcers and low-fibre problems.',a:'1. Eat a high-fibre diet (vegetables, fruit, pandanus, whole grains) and drink plenty of water.\n2. Avoid excessive alcohol, smoking and spicy or fatty foods; eat smaller, regular meals. (Also: treat <i>H. pylori</i> infection.)'},
{n:'3B.',q:'How does a gastric ulcer affect the digestive system?',a:'The ulcer is an open sore in the stomach lining. Stomach acid irritates it, causing pain and possible bleeding. It reduces digestion of food (especially protein) and can lead to serious complications such as a perforated stomach.'},
{n:'4A.',q:'Where do ingestion and digestion occur?',a:'Ingestion: the <b>mouth</b>.\nDigestion: the <b>stomach and small intestine</b> (it starts in the mouth with amylase).'},
{n:'4B.',q:'One function of the oesophagus.',a:'It carries food from the mouth to the stomach by peristalsis (waves of muscle contraction).'},
{n:'4C.',q:'One herbivore adaptation for digesting plant material.',a:'A long alimentary canal with a rumen or large caecum containing microbes (bacteria) that produce cellulase to digest cellulose. (Also accepted: flat grinding molars.)'},
{n:'4D.',q:'One advantage of a tube gut over a sac gut.',a:'Food moves one way, so different regions can specialise (digestion, absorption, egestion), and the animal can keep eating while earlier food is still being digested.'},
{n:'5A.',q:'Gas exchange organ: (i) earthworm (ii) insects.',a:'(i) Moist <b>skin</b>\n(ii) <b>Tracheal system</b> (spiracles and tracheae)'},
{n:'5B.',q:'One adaptation of fish for efficient gas exchange.',a:'Countercurrent flow: water flows over the gills in the opposite direction to the blood, keeping a concentration gradient along the whole gill. (Or: many thin lamellae giving a large surface area.)'},
{n:'5C.',q:'One similarity between gas exchange in fish and insects.',a:'Both have a very large surface area (gill lamellae / tracheoles) with thin walls for fast diffusion of O₂ and CO₂.'},
{n:'5D.',q:'Why do earthworms rely on diffusion through the skin?',a:'Earthworms have no lungs or gills. They are small and thin with a large surface area to volume ratio and a low metabolic rate. Their skin is thin and moist, with many capillaries underneath, so diffusion through the skin supplies enough oxygen.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Open vs closed circulatory systems: (B) structural differences (C) one advantage of closed.',a:'B. Open: the blood is not kept in vessels; it is pumped into a body cavity and bathes the organs directly, at low pressure. Closed: the blood stays inside vessels (arteries, capillaries, veins) and is pumped by the heart at high pressure.\nC. A closed system delivers oxygen and nutrients faster and can direct blood to the organs that need it most. This suits larger, more active animals.'},
{n:'7.1.',q:'Fertilisation to implantation: (A) structure X (B) define implantation and give hormones (C) role of process 1.',a:'A. <b>Zygote</b> (the fertilised egg).\nB. Implantation: the embryo (blastocyst) attaches and embeds into the thickened lining of the uterus. Hormones: <b>progesterone</b> and <b>oestrogen</b>.\nC. Process 1 = <b>ovulation</b>: the release of a mature egg from the ovary into the oviduct, so it can be fertilised.'},
{n:'7.2.',q:'Placenta: (A) part (iii) (B) functions of (i) and (ii) (C) two other functions.',a:'A. <b>Umbilical cord</b>.\nB. (i) Umbilical vein: carries oxygenated, nutrient-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus. (ii) Umbilical artery: carries deoxygenated blood and wastes (CO₂, urea) from the fetus to the placenta.\nC. 1. It produces hormones (progesterone, oestrogen) that maintain the pregnancy. 2. It passes the mother\'s antibodies to the fetus (passive immunity) and acts as a barrier to some harmful substances and microbes.'}
]},
{t:'B5 – Environmental biology (28 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Dichotomous key: identify the four insects.',a:'Per the marking scheme: 2a → IV, 2b → II, 3a → III, 3b → I. Follow each pair of statements in the key and choose the one that matches each insect.'},
{n:'2A–B.',q:'Define population size; two estimation methods.',a:'Population size: the total number of individuals of one species living in an area at a particular time.\nMethods: 1. Quadrat sampling (count in sample squares and scale up). 2. Mark–release–recapture (N = first catch × second catch ÷ marked in second catch).'},
{n:'2D.',q:'Define natality and mortality.',a:'Natality: the birth rate (new individuals added by reproduction per unit time).\nMortality: the death rate (individuals lost through death per unit time).'},
{n:'2E.',q:'An example of a Type I and a Type II organism.',a:'Type I: humans (or elephants) – most survive to old age.\nType II: birds (or lizards, rodents) – a roughly constant death rate throughout life.'},
{n:'2F.',q:'How natality and mortality produce the Type III curve.',a:'Type III organisms (e.g. fish, oysters, coral) have very high natality: they release huge numbers of eggs or seeds. Mortality is very high at the early stage (predation, no parental care), so the curve drops steeply at first. The few survivors have low mortality and live a long time, so the curve then flattens.'},
{n:'3A.',q:'(i) Ecological term for the change in vegetation with altitude (ii) the effect of global warming.',a:'(i) <b>Zonation</b>.\n(ii) As temperatures rise, each vegetation zone shifts to higher altitudes. Species adapted to the cold top zones lose habitat and may become extinct, and crops may need to be grown higher up.'},
{n:'4.1.',q:'Pyramids of numbers and biomass: difference, limitation, shape, why biomass is upright, human impact.',a:'A. A pyramid of numbers counts the organisms at each level; a pyramid of biomass shows the dry mass of organisms at each level.\nB. A pyramid of numbers ignores the size of organisms (one tree = one), so it does not show energy flow accurately.\nC. It is not upright because one large producer (a tree) can support many small herbivores (insects), so the second bar is wider than the first.\nD. On land, energy and biomass are lost at each level (respiration, heat, uneaten parts, waste), so each level has less biomass than the one below.\nE. Deforestation or overfishing removes producers or consumers and reduces biomass. (Also: pollution, climate change, coral bleaching.)'},
{n:'4.2.',q:'(A) Define biodiversity (B) one human activity that reduces it (C) why high biodiversity matters.',a:'A. The variety of living things in an area: species diversity, genetic diversity and ecosystem diversity.\nB. Overfishing, habitat destruction (e.g. clearing mangroves, sand mining), pollution, or introducing invasive species.\nC. Many species give an ecosystem stability: if one species declines, others can fill its role in the food web. It also provides services (pollination, nutrient cycling, coastal protection by reefs) and resources (food, medicines).'}
]},
{t:'Section C – Extended responses (2 × 10 marks)', qs:[
{n:'C1.',q:'How pH, temperature, surface area and substrate concentration affect enzyme-controlled reactions (refer to specificity, optimum conditions, denaturation).',a:'<b>Introduction:</b> Enzymes are protein biological catalysts that speed up reactions by lowering the activation energy. Each has an active site with a specific shape.\n<b>Specificity:</b> only a substrate with a complementary shape can bind to the active site (induced fit), forming an enzyme–substrate complex.\n<b>Temperature:</b> as temperature rises, molecules move faster, so there are more collisions and the rate increases up to the optimum (~37 °C in humans). Above the optimum, hydrogen bonds in the enzyme break, the active site changes shape and the enzyme is <b>denatured</b>, so the rate falls sharply.\n<b>pH:</b> each enzyme has an optimum pH (pepsin ~2, salivary amylase ~7). Away from it, charges in the active site change; extreme pH denatures the enzyme.\n<b>Surface area:</b> a larger substrate surface area (e.g. chewed food) exposes more substrate to the enzymes, so there are more collisions and a faster rate.\n<b>Substrate concentration:</b> the rate increases with concentration until all active sites are occupied (saturation), then levels off.\n<b>Conclusion:</b> enzymes work fastest under optimum conditions. Conditions that change the shape of the active site reduce activity, and denaturation is permanent.'},
{n:'C2.',q:'How the liver maintains blood glucose: the roles of the pancreas, insulin and glucagon; Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes.',a:'<b>Introduction:</b> Blood glucose must be kept near 4–7 mmol/L (homeostasis) by negative feedback.\n<b>Pancreas:</b> the islets of Langerhans detect blood glucose. β cells release insulin when glucose is high; α cells release glucagon when it is low.\n<b>Insulin:</b> after a meal, insulin makes liver and muscle cells take up glucose and convert it to <b>glycogen</b> (glycogenesis), lowering blood glucose.\n<b>Glucagon:</b> between meals or during exercise, glucagon makes the liver break glycogen down into glucose (glycogenolysis) and release it into the blood, raising blood glucose.\n<b>Diabetes:</b> Type 1: the immune system destroys the β cells, so little or no insulin is made; it is treated with insulin injections. Type 2: body cells become resistant to insulin (linked to obesity, sugary food and lack of exercise). It is very common in Kiribati and is managed by diet, exercise and medicine. In both types, blood glucose stays high, damaging the eyes, kidneys, nerves and feet.\n<b>Conclusion:</b> the liver, pancreas, insulin and glucagon work together to keep glucose balanced. A healthy lifestyle helps prevent Type 2 diabetes.'}
]}
]},
{y:2024, src:'P2024/2024 KSSC Biology Exam.pdf', note:'Worked answers, checked against the official marking scheme. ⚠ marks the many places where the MOE scheme or the question is wrong.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Multiple choice (40 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'One species benefits and the other is not harmed',a:'<b>D</b>: commensalism.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Earthworm skin as a gas-exchange surface',a:'<b>D</b>: large surface area and thin skin (short diffusion distance). It is also kept moist.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Absorption, assimilation, egestion',a:'<b>A</b>. Absorption: small soluble molecules move into the blood. Assimilation: they are used to build body compounds. Egestion: undigested food leaves through the anus.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Which statement about bile is NOT true?',a:'MOE answer: <b>A</b> (bile emulsifies <i>fats</i>, not proteins).\n⚠ The question is faulty: B (bile has <b>no enzymes</b>) and D (bile neutralises <b>acid</b> from the stomach, not alkali) are also false. Only C is true. In the exam, choose A.'},
{n:'5.',q:'NOT a function of the liver',a:'<b>D</b>: secreting digestive enzymes (that is the pancreas).'},
{n:'6.',q:'Hormone that grows the uterus lining',a:'<b>B</b>: oestrogen rebuilds the lining. Progesterone then <i>maintains</i> it.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Correct sequence in a plant life cycle',a:'<b>D</b>: zygote → sporophyte → meiosis → spores.\n⚠ The MOE scheme says B, which is wrong: spores grow into the gametophyte by <i>mitosis</i>, not meiosis. A is wrong too (spores are made by meiosis), and so is C (gametophytes make gametes by mitosis).'},
{n:'8.',q:'Number of individuals per unit area',a:'<b>C</b>: population density.'},
{n:'9.',q:'Sexual vs asexual reproduction in plants',a:'<b>A</b>: sexual needs two parents, asexual needs only one.'},
{n:'10.',q:'Root hairs and absorption',a:'<b>B</b>: a large surface area; water enters by osmosis and mineral ions by active transport.'},
{n:'11.',q:'What do bacteria in legume root nodules do?',a:'<b>D</b>: nitrogen fixation (N₂ → ammonium compounds).\n⚠ The MOE scheme says A (nitrification), which is wrong. Nitrification is done by soil bacteria, not by root-nodule bacteria.'},
{n:'12.',q:'Function of the endoplasmic reticulum',a:'<b>C</b>: transporting materials through the cell. Packaging is the Golgi body, and destroying old parts is the lysosomes.'},
{n:'13.',q:'Offspring who look nothing like their parents',a:'<b>A</b>: both parents are Gg (they show the dominant trait), and the child gets gg (the recessive trait).'},
{n:'14.',q:'Crossing over is an exchange between…',a:'<b>C</b>: non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.'},
{n:'15.',q:'Changes in a community over time',a:'<b>B</b>: succession.'},
{n:'16.',q:'Smallest to largest',a:'<b>A</b>: gene → DNA strand → chromosome → nucleus.'},
{n:'17.',q:'Enzyme graph: X (rising part), Y (after the peak)',a:'<b>B</b>: at X the rate is increasing, and at Y the enzyme is becoming denatured.'},
{n:'18.',q:'Root hair cell: cell wall, membrane, mitochondria',a:'<b>A</b>: support, active transport, energy release.'},
{n:'19.',q:'Open vs closed circulatory systems',a:'MOE answer: <b>A</b>.\n⚠ The question is faulty: <b>every option has the two columns swapped</b>. In fact, in an <b>open</b> system the blood and tissue fluid are mixed, the blood is pumped into a body cavity, it bathes the tissues directly, and it flows slowly. A <b>closed</b> system is the opposite.'},
{n:'20.',q:'Structures ALWAYS visible in plant cells under a light microscope',a:'<b>C</b>: the nucleus and the vacuole only. Ribosomes are too small, and not all plant cells (e.g. root cells) have chloroplasts.\n⚠ The MOE scheme says B (1, 3 and 4), but chloroplasts are not in every plant cell.'}
]},
{t:'B1 – Cellular biology (28 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Order from largest to smallest: tissue, organism, cell, system, organ',a:'Organism (1) → System (2) → Organ (3) → Tissue (4) → Cell (5)\nSo: Tissue = <b>4</b>, Organism = <b>1</b>, Cell = <b>5</b>, System = <b>2</b>, Organ = <b>3</b>\n(⚠ The table on the paper says tissue is "2". That is part of the printed example, but it is not correct for largest → smallest.)'},
{n:'2.',q:'Organelle table',a:'(a) Ribosomes: <b>make proteins</b> (protein synthesis)\nProtects and supports the cell, plants only: <b>cell wall</b>\n(b) Centrioles: <b>animal cells</b>'},
{n:'3(i).',q:'Equation for aerobic respiration',a:'<b>C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O</b> (+ energy as ATP)'},
{n:'3(ii).',q:'Two products of anaerobic respiration in yeast',a:'<b>Ethanol (alcohol) and carbon dioxide</b> (plus a little energy). This is fermentation, used in bread and toddy.'},
{n:'4(i).',q:'Acidic or alkaline: which is best for starch digestion? (Table 1)',a:'Digestion is fastest at <b>pH 7 (7 min)</b>, so a <b>neutral to slightly alkaline</b> environment is best (pH 8 takes 8 min). In acid it is much slower: 38 min at pH 3. Amylase (from saliva and the pancreas) works best around neutral.\n⚠ The MOE scheme says "pH 8 is fastest", but the table shows pH 7 is fastest.'},
{n:'4(ii).',q:'Effect of temperature (Table 2)',a:'From 10 °C to <b>40 °C</b> the time falls from 20 to 6 min, so digestion gets <b>faster</b> (more kinetic energy, more collisions). 40 °C is the <b>optimum</b>. Above 40 °C the time rises again (23 min at 60 °C) because the enzyme is <b>denatured</b> (its active site changes shape).\n⚠ The MOE scheme says only "slower as temperature increases", which is wrong below 40 °C.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Cellular respiration diagram',a:'(i) X = <b>glycolysis</b> (in the cytoplasm)\n(ii) Krebs cycle: in the <b>matrix</b> of the mitochondria\n(iii) Glycolysis: <b>2 ATP</b> (net). Electron transport: <b>about 32–34 ATP</b>.\n(iv) A = <b>oxygen</b>, Y = <b>water</b>'},
{n:'6.',q:'Photosynthesis',a:'(i) The dark reaction (Calvin cycle) happens in the <b>stroma</b>.\n(ii) <b>ATP</b> (or NADPH) from the light reaction.\n(iii) <b>Glucose</b> (sugar).\n(iv) <b>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</b> (light energy, chlorophyll)'}
]},
{t:'B2 – Skills (14 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1(i).',q:'Functions of microscope parts A (top) and B',a:'A: <b>eyepiece lens</b>, which you look through; it magnifies the image.\nB: <b>stage</b>, which holds the slide with the specimen.'},
{n:'1(ii).',q:'10× eyepiece and 100× objective',a:'Total magnification = eyepiece × objective = 10 × 100 = <b>1000×</b>'},
{n:'1(iii).',q:'One advantage and one disadvantage of a light microscope',a:'Advantage: it can view <b>living</b> cells and colour; it is cheap and easy to use.\nDisadvantage: <b>low magnification and resolution</b> (up to about 1500×), so small organelles such as ribosomes cannot be seen.'},
{n:'2(i).',q:'Describe the trend in the potato mass graph',a:'The mass <b>increases steadily</b> for about the first 20 minutes, because water enters by osmosis. Then it <b>levels off</b> at about 0.31 g (no further change), because the solution inside and outside the cells reaches the same concentration and the cells are full of water (turgid).'},
{n:'2(ii).',q:'Initial mass 2.5 g. % increase after 15 min',a:'Increase at 15 min ≈ 0.25 g (from the graph)\n% increase = 0.25/2.5 × 100 = <b>10%</b>'},
{n:'2(iii–iv).',q:'Draw and explain the line at 30 °C',a:'Draw a line that is <b>steeper at the start</b> and <b>levels off sooner</b> (it may reach about the same final mass).\nReason: a higher temperature gives the water molecules <b>more kinetic energy</b>, so they move faster and <b>osmosis is faster</b>.'}
]},
{t:'B3 – Genetics (19 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1(i).',q:'Define selective breeding',a:'When <b>humans choose parents</b> with useful characteristics and breed them together, over many generations, so the offspring have those wanted traits (artificial selection).'},
{n:'1(ii).',q:'Why do flower farmers use selective breeding?',a:'To get flowers with traits that sell well: <b>bright or new colours, a strong scent, flowers that last longer</b>, faster growth, or resistance to disease.'},
{n:'2(a).',q:'Define recessive allele',a:'An allele that is <b>only expressed when there are two copies</b> (homozygous). It is hidden when a dominant allele is present.'},
{n:'2(b).',q:'Genotypes: heterozygous spotted non-wooly man × heterozygous wooly non-spotted woman',a:'Man: <b>Ssww</b>\nWoman: <b>ssWw</b>'},
{n:'2(c).',q:'Punnet square and phenotype ratio',a:'Man\'s gametes: Sw, sw. Woman\'s gametes: sW, sw.\n\n|    | Sw   | sw   |\n| sW | SsWw | ssWw |\n| sw | Ssww | ssww |\n\nSsWw: spotted, wooly\nSsww: spotted, non-wooly\nssWw: non-spotted, wooly\nssww: non-spotted, non-wooly\n<b>Ratio 1 : 1 : 1 : 1</b>'},
{n:'3(a).',q:'Chromosomes in a goat gamete',a:'60 ÷ 2 = <b>30</b>. Gametes are haploid.'},
{n:'3(b).',q:'Why do most organisms have an even number of chromosomes?',a:'Chromosomes come in <b>homologous pairs</b>: one set from the mother and one set from the father.'},
{n:'3(c).',q:'Function of DNA',a:'DNA carries the <b>genetic code (genes)</b>, the instructions for <b>making proteins</b>, which control the cell\'s characteristics. It is passed on to offspring.'},
{n:'3(d).',q:'(i) Why is cell division important? (ii) Chromosomes in new potato cells',a:'(i) For <b>growth, repair</b> of damaged tissue, and replacing old cells (and asexual reproduction).\n(ii) <b>44</b>. Mitosis keeps the same number.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Protein synthesis diagram',a:'(i) A = <b>nucleus</b>, B = <b>cytoplasm (ribosome)</b>\n(ii) X = <b>transcription</b>: the DNA code of a gene is <b>copied into mRNA</b>, which carries it out of the nucleus to the ribosome for translation.'}
]},
{t:'B4 – Organism-level biology (51 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Water transport tissue; stem diagram; monocot or dicot?',a:'1. <b>Xylem</b> (phloem carries sugars).\n2(i). Put X on the <b>inner side of each vascular bundle</b> (xylem is towards the centre, phloem towards the outside).\n2(ii). <b>Dicot</b>: the vascular bundles are arranged in a <b>ring</b>. (In a monocot they are scattered.)'},
{n:'2.',q:'Alternation of generations diagram',a:'(i) Y = <b>fertilisation</b>, Z = <b>meiosis</b>\n(ii) X = <b>zygote</b>\n(iii) Write S on the large plant that grows from the zygote (the <b>sporophyte</b>). Write 2n on the zygote–sporophyte side, from fertilisation round to meiosis.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Predict transpiration (0–5) at high and low temperature and humidity',a:'Temperature: high → <b>4–5</b>, low → <b>0–1</b>. Warmer air means faster evaporation and diffusion.\nAir humidity: high → <b>0–1</b>, low → <b>4–5</b>. Dry air gives a steeper water-vapour gradient, so transpiration is faster.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Umbilical cord: X and Y; which carries O₂; functions of the placenta (Z)',a:'(i) X = <b>umbilical vein</b>, Y = <b>umbilical artery</b>\n(ii) <b>X</b>: the vein carries oxygenated blood from the placenta to the foetus.\n(iii) The placenta (Z): 1. lets <b>O₂ and nutrients</b> pass from the mother\'s blood to the foetus; 2. removes <b>CO₂ and wastes</b> (urea) from the foetus. It also makes hormones (progesterone) and blocks many germs.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Blood glucose control',a:'(i) Something that lowers blood glucose: <b>exercise</b> (glucose used in respiration), skipping meals, or too much insulin.\n(ii) <b>Negative feedback</b>:\n• Glucose <b>high</b> (after a meal) → the pancreas releases <b>insulin</b> → the liver converts glucose into <b>glycogen</b> (stored) and cells take up glucose → the level falls back to normal.\n• Glucose <b>low</b> → the pancreas releases <b>glucagon</b> → the liver breaks glycogen down into <b>glucose</b> → the level rises back to normal.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Gut 1 (earthworm tube) vs Gut 2 (sea anemone sac)',a:'(i) <b>Gut 2</b> is the sac gut.\n(ii) C = <b>tentacles</b>: they catch prey (with stinging cells) and push the food into the mouth.\n(iii) Gut 1 is a <b>tube gut with two openings</b> (mouth and anus), so food moves one way. Gut 2 has <b>one opening</b>, used both to take in food and to get rid of waste.\n(iv) A sac gut is <b>less efficient</b>: food and waste use the same opening, it cannot eat continuously, and it has no specialised regions. That is fine for a <b>sessile (fixed), slow</b> animal in water that only catches food now and then and needs little energy.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Gas exchange system',a:'(i) W = <b>trachea</b>, Y = <b>bronchus/bronchiole</b>\n(ii) Alveoli: 1. a <b>huge surface area</b>; 2. walls <b>one cell thin</b> (short diffusion path). They are also moist and surrounded by many capillaries.\nThe structure that helps move the ribs: the <b>diaphragm</b> (Z) works with the <b>intercostal muscles</b>, which actually move the ribs.\n(iii) Oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood and is <b>used by the cells in respiration</b>, so expired air has less O₂ (about 16% compared with 21%).\n(iv) <b>Aerobic respiration</b>.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Obesity and coronary heart disease',a:'(i) Obesity: having <b>too much body fat</b> (BMI over 30). Fatty diets raise cholesterol, which builds up as <b>fatty plaques in the coronary arteries</b>. This narrows them, so less O₂ reaches the heart muscle and can cause a heart attack. Extra weight also raises blood pressure.\n(ii) Two other factors: <b>smoking</b>, <b>lack of exercise</b>, a diet high in fat or salt, high blood pressure, too much alcohol, stress, or family history.\n⚠ The MOE scheme lists the causes of <i>obesity</i>, which does not answer this question.'},
{n:'9.',q:'Excretion and urine',a:'(i) Lungs: <b>carbon dioxide</b>. Kidneys: <b>urea</b> (and excess salts).\n(ii) More water drunk → volume <b>increases</b>, concentration <b>decreases</b>.\nMore exercise (sweating) → volume <b>decreases</b>, concentration <b>increases</b>.\n(iii) <b>Gout</b> is caused by too much <b>uric acid</b> in the blood, often from a diet rich in purines (red meat, seafood, beer). The uric acid forms <b>sharp crystals in the joints</b> (often the big toe), causing painful, swollen, red joints that make walking hard. It can also cause kidney stones.'},
{n:'10.',q:'Nitrogenous waste',a:'(i) Birds: <b>uric acid</b>. Fish: <b>ammonia</b>.\n(ii) A (ammonia) is <b>very toxic</b> and very soluble, so it must be diluted with lots of water before it is released. Fish live in water, so this is easy for them.'}
]},
{t:'B5 – Environmental biology (28 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1(a).',q:'Genus of the great white shark (Carcharodon carcharias)',a:'<b>Carcharodon</b> (the first name, with a capital letter).'},
{n:'1(b).',q:'Two adaptations of the shark',a:'1. <b>Streamlined body</b>: less drag, so it swims fast to catch prey.\n2. <b>Fins and a strong tail</b>: power, steering and balance.\n(Also: rows of <b>sharp teeth</b> for gripping prey; <b>countershading</b> (dark on top, white underneath) for camouflage; gills to take O₂ from the water.)'},
{n:'1(c).',q:'A biotic and an abiotic factor affecting shark populations',a:'Biotic (living): <b>overfishing by humans</b> (shark finning), disease, less prey.\nAbiotic (non-living): <b>rising sea temperature</b>, pollution, ocean acidity.'},
{n:'1(d).',q:'Food chain with four trophic levels',a:'e.g. <b>phytoplankton → zooplankton → small fish → shark</b>. The arrows show the direction energy flows.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Energy pyramid',a:'1. Level 1 = <b>producers</b>. Level 3 = <b>secondary consumers (carnivores)</b>.\n2. Level 1: <b>phytoplankton/seaweed</b>. Level 4: <b>shark or tuna</b> (top or tertiary consumer).\n3. At each level about <b>90% of the energy is lost</b> as heat from respiration, in movement, and in undigested waste and uneaten parts. Only about 10% passes up, so each level is smaller.\n4. Energy flows <b>one way</b>, from producers to consumers, when organisms are eaten. Only about 10% is transferred each time, which is why food chains rarely have more than 4–5 levels.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Carbon graph',a:'(a) X = <b>photosynthesis</b> (it removes CO₂).\n(b) Added: respiration by plants (50) + animals (60) + microorganisms (30) + combustion (5) ≈ <b>145</b> billion tonnes. Removed by photosynthesis ≈ <b>135</b> billion tonnes. More carbon is <b>added than removed</b> (about 10 billion tonnes a year), so the carbon in the atmosphere is <b>increasing</b> (global warming).'},
{n:'3(1).',q:'Gause\'s principle and Liebig\'s law',a:'<b>Gause\'s principle (competitive exclusion)</b>: two species competing for exactly the same limited resource (the same niche) <b>cannot live together for long</b>. One wins and the other dies out or moves.\n<b>Liebig\'s law of the minimum</b>: growth is controlled by the <b>scarcest resource (the limiting factor)</b>, not by the total of all resources.'},
{n:'3(2).',q:'Dichotomous key for leaves a–d',a:'1a. Leaf has smooth edges → go to 2\n1b. Leaf has rough (toothed) edges → go to 3\n2a. Single leaf, oval/round → <b>d</b>\n2b. Compound leaf (many small leaflets) → <b>c</b>\n3a. Long, thin leaf → <b>b</b>\n3b. Short, broad leaf → <b>a</b>\n(Any key that splits the four leaves using one feature per step gets the marks.)'},
{n:'4.',q:'Causes and an example of genetic variation',a:'(i) 1. <b>Mutation</b> (a change in the DNA). 2. <b>Sexual reproduction</b>: meiosis (crossing over, independent assortment) and random fertilisation. (Gene flow from migration is another.)\n(ii) Examples: different <b>coat colours</b> in dogs or cats, albinism, blood groups in humans.'}
]},
{t:'Section C – Extended responses (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'C1.',q:'Effect of osmosis on plant and animal cells (isotonic and hypertonic)',a:'<b>Osmosis</b>: the movement of water molecules from a dilute solution (high water potential) to a concentrated solution (low water potential) through a partially permeable membrane.\n<b>Isotonic</b> solution (the same concentration as the cell): no net water movement. The animal cell stays normal; the plant cell stays the same (slightly flaccid).\n<b>Hypertonic</b> solution (more concentrated outside): water leaves the cell. The animal cell (e.g. a red blood cell) <b>shrinks and crinkles (crenation)</b>. In a plant cell the vacuole shrinks and the membrane pulls away from the wall: <b>plasmolysis</b>. The cell wall stops the plant cell from collapsing completely.'},
{n:'C2.',q:'Genetic modification: definition, examples, advantages and disadvantages',a:'<b>Genetic modification (GM)</b>: changing an organism\'s DNA in a laboratory, usually by <b>inserting a gene from another organism</b>.\nExamples: 1. Bacteria given the human gene to make <b>insulin</b> for diabetics. 2. <b>Pest-resistant crops</b> (Bt maize) or vitamin-rich <b>golden rice</b>.\nAdvantage: more food with fewer pesticides; cheap medicines.\nDisadvantage: genes may spread to wild plants; possible unknown health or ecological effects; costly seeds owned by big companies.'},
{n:'C3.',q:'Traditional Pacific diet vs modern diet',a:'<b>Traditional diet</b>: fresh local food from land and sea, e.g. <b>fish, breadfruit, babai, pandanus, coconut</b>. High in fibre and vitamins, low in sugar and salt.\n<b>Modern diet</b>: imported, processed food, e.g. <b>rice, flour, sugar, canned meat, noodles, soft drinks</b>. High in fat, salt and sugar, with preservatives.\n<b>Difference</b>: traditional food is fresh and unprocessed, while modern food is processed and energy-dense. The modern diet is linked to more <b>obesity, diabetes and heart disease</b> in Kiribati.'},
{n:'C4.',q:'One human activity that harms coral reefs, and its effect on biodiversity',a:'<b>Biodiversity</b>: the variety of different species (and genes and ecosystems) in an area.\n<b>Ecosystem</b>: a community of living things interacting with each other and their non-living environment.\nHuman impact: e.g. <b>overfishing</b>, coral mining, sewage pollution, or global warming causing <b>coral bleaching</b>.\nEffect: corals are the habitat and food source for many fish and invertebrates. When the coral dies, these species lose their homes and food, populations fall and some disappear, so <b>biodiversity decreases</b>. The food web becomes unstable, which also affects people who rely on reef fish.'}
]}
]},
{y:2023, src:'P2023/BIOLOGY EXAM 2023.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the MOE marking scheme. ⚠ marks the places where the scheme is wrong. The official multiple-choice key does not match this paper for several questions; the correct answers are given here.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–5',q:'Enzyme; diffusion diagram; rough ER; not a light-reaction product; respiration',a:'1 <b>A</b> a protein biological catalyst (not used up)\n2 <b>C</b> active transport – the carrier protein uses ATP to move particles against the gradient\n3 <b>A</b> rough ER: protein synthesis (ribosomes)\n4 <b>B</b> glucose is made in the Calvin cycle, not the light reactions\n5 <b>C</b> ADP + phosphate → ATP\n⚠ The MOE key gives 1 D, 2 B, 4 C and 5 A, which are wrong.'},
{n:'6–10',q:'Building blocks of DNA; DNA–gene–chromosome; phenotype; selective breeding; wood',a:'6 <b>C</b> nucleotides · 7 <b>B</b> · 8 <b>B</b> phenotype · 9 <b>D</b> artificial selection · 10 <b>D</b> secondary xylem\n⚠ The MOE key gives 8 C and 10 A, which are wrong.'},
{n:'11–15',q:'Fern meiosis; main digestion; tracheal system; gastrovascular cavity; Cnidaria transport',a:'11 <b>D</b> sporophyte (diploid; its sporangia make spores by meiosis) · 12 <b>C</b> small intestine · 13 <b>C</b> grasshoppers · 14 <b>D</b> coral polyp · 15 <b>C</b> thin body layers – diffusion is enough\n⚠ The MOE key gives 11 C, 13 B and 15 A, which are wrong.'},
{n:'16–20',q:'Not a waste; menstruation hormone; sea stars; adaptation; lichen',a:'16 <b>B</b> amino acids are not excreted · 17 <b>D</b> menstruation follows the fall in progesterone · 18 <b>C</b> echinoderms · 19 <b>D</b> adaptation · 20 <b>A</b> mutualism'}
]},
{t:'B1 Part A – Cell biology', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Lock and key model',a:'The enzyme\'s <b>active site</b> has a specific shape that fits only one substrate, like a key fits a lock. The substrate binds to form an <b>enzyme–substrate complex</b>; the reaction happens; the <b>products</b> leave and the enzyme is unchanged and can be used again.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Blood enzyme activity vs pH; denatured; distorted active site',a:'Activity rises as pH increases up to the <b>optimum pH</b> (about 7.4 for a blood enzyme), where it is fastest. Above (or below) the optimum, activity falls quickly to zero.\nA <b>denatured enzyme</b> has lost its 3-D shape (bonds holding it are broken) and no longer works – the change is permanent.\nA <b>distorted active site</b> has changed shape, so the substrate no longer fits and no enzyme–substrate complexes form.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Process; plant cell in dilute and concentrated solutions',a:'A. <b>Osmosis</b>\nB. (i) Less concentrated (dilute) solution: water enters → <b>turgid</b>.\n(ii) Concentrated solution: water leaves → <b>plasmolysed</b> (flaccid).\n⚠ The MOE scheme swaps (i) and (ii), which is wrong.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Biconcave shape of red blood cells',a:'The biconcave disc gives a <b>large surface area to volume ratio</b>, so O₂ diffuses in and out quickly; the cell is <b>thin</b>, so the diffusion distance is short; it is <b>flexible</b>, so it can squeeze through narrow capillaries. (No nucleus also leaves more room for haemoglobin.)'},
{n:'5.',q:'Plant cell: Q; two parts in both plant and animal cells; P and R',a:'Q = <b>cytoplasm</b>.\nA. Found in both: <b>nucleus</b>, <b>cytoplasm</b>, cell membrane, mitochondria (any two).\nB. P = <b>chloroplast</b> – absorbs light for photosynthesis. R = <b>large (permanent) vacuole</b> – stores cell sap and keeps the cell turgid for support.'}
]},
{t:'B1 Part B – Photosynthesis experiment', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Moll\'s half-leaf: purpose of KOH; conclusion; equation',a:'A. KOH <b>absorbs carbon dioxide</b> from the air in the bottle.\nB. The part of leaf A inside the bottle gave a <b>negative</b> starch test, the part outside a positive one → <b>CO₂ is needed for photosynthesis</b>.\nC. 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ (light, chlorophyll)'},
{n:'2.',q:'Structures 1–3; why light reactions come first',a:'A. 1 = <b>grana</b> (thylakoids); 2 = chloroplast <b>membrane/envelope</b>; 3 = <b>stroma</b>.\nB. The light reactions use light energy to split water, releasing O₂ and making <b>ATP and NADPH</b>. The Calvin cycle needs this ATP (energy) and NADPH (hydrogen) to reduce CO₂ to sugar, so it can only run once the light reactions have supplied them.'}
]},
{t:'B1 Part C – Respiration', qs:[
{n:'1–3.',q:'Where the ETC is; processes A, B, C; summary of C',a:'1. X on the <b>inner membrane (cristae) of the mitochondrion</b>.\n2. A = <b>glycolysis</b> (cytoplasm); B = <b>Krebs cycle</b> (matrix); C = <b>electron transport chain</b>.\n3. NADH and FADH₂ give electrons to the chain → the energy pumps H⁺ ions across the inner membrane → H⁺ flow back through <b>ATP synthase</b>, making about 34 ATP → oxygen accepts the electrons and H⁺ to form <b>water</b>.'}
]},
{t:'B2 – Genetics and inheritance', qs:[
{n:'A1–2.',q:'Gene; transcription and translation',a:'1. A gene is a section of DNA on a chromosome that carries the code (base sequence) for making one protein.\n2a. <b>Transcription</b> (nucleus): DNA unzips; one strand is used as a template to make a complementary <b>mRNA</b> copy of the gene; mRNA leaves through a nuclear pore.\nb. <b>Translation</b> (ribosome): the ribosome reads mRNA codons; <b>tRNA</b> brings the matching amino acids (anticodon pairs with codon); amino acids are joined by peptide bonds to make the polypeptide.'},
{n:'A3.',q:'Onion root tip: anaphase; chromosomes in daughter cells',a:'A. X on a cell where the chromosomes are being pulled to opposite poles (two V-shaped groups).\nB. Mitosis keeps the number the same: <b>16 chromosomes</b> in each daughter cell.'},
{n:'A4.',q:'Crossing over; recombination; independent assortment',a:'Crossing over: non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes swap sections in meiosis I → new combinations of alleles on a chromosome.\nRecombination: produces new combinations of alleles in gametes (from crossing over and independent assortment) → variation.\nIndependent assortment: homologous pairs line up randomly at metaphase I, so alleles of different genes go into gametes independently.'},
{n:'B1.',q:'Guinea pigs: recessive; parents; cross',a:'A. A recessive allele is only expressed when two copies are present (homozygous); it is hidden by the dominant allele.\nB. P1 = <b>BBLL</b>; P2 = <b>bbLL</b>\nC. Gametes: BL × bL → all offspring <b>BbLL</b> → <b>100% black, long hair</b> (ratio 1 : 0).'},
{n:'B2–3.',q:'Purpose and benefits of selective breeding',a:'2. Humans choose parents with desirable characteristics and breed them over many generations to produce offspring with those traits.\n3. Crops: higher yield, resistance to pests/disease/drought or salt (e.g. better giant swamp taro, breadfruit). Animals: faster growth, more meat, eggs or milk (e.g. broiler and layer chickens, pigs).'}
]},
{t:'B3 Part A – Plant biology', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Plant types A, B; structures X and Y',a:'A. A = <b>dicot</b>; B = <b>monocot</b>.\nB. X = <b>vascular bundle</b> – xylem carries water and minerals, phloem carries sugars. Y = <b>root hair</b> – large surface area to absorb water and minerals.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Light and water effects on transpiration; role of guard cells',a:'A. (i) More light → stomata open → <b>faster</b> transpiration. (ii) Plenty of water → stomata stay open → faster; in water shortage stomata close → <b>slower</b>.\nB. In light (and when water is plentiful) guard cells take in water and become <b>turgid</b>; their thick inner walls make them bend apart and the stoma opens. When water is short they become <b>flaccid</b> and the stoma closes, reducing water loss.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Fern life cycle',a:'A. X (diploid) on the sporophyte/fern plant or zygote; Z (haploid) on the spores or gametophyte (prothallus).\nB. Huge numbers of light spores spread far by wind; the gametophyte can self-fertilise in a film of water.\nC. The life cycle alternates between a diploid <b>sporophyte</b> (makes spores by meiosis) and a haploid <b>gametophyte</b> (makes gametes by mitosis).\nD. Ferns have <b>vascular tissue</b> (xylem and phloem) and true roots, stems and leaves with a cuticle, so they can grow taller and transport water; mosses have no vascular tissue.'}
]},
{t:'B3 Part B – Gas exchange and nutrition', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Insect and amphibian gas exchange; efficiency',a:'A. (i) Insect: <b>tracheal system</b> – spiracles → tracheae → tracheoles take air straight to the cells. (ii) Amphibian: <b>lungs and moist skin</b> (and mouth lining).\nB. E.g. insect: air reaches every cell directly, so no blood is needed to carry O₂ – very efficient for a small, active, flying animal; spiracles can close to save water on land. (Amphibian: skin must stay moist, which ties frogs to damp places; lungs help when active.)'},
{n:'2.',q:'Lifestyle diseases; obese; fibre and diabetes',a:'A. (i) Non-communicable diseases caused mainly by the way people live – poor diet, inactivity, smoking, alcohol (e.g. type 2 diabetes, heart disease). (ii) Obese = having too much body fat (BMI 30 or more).\nB. (The question should say a <b>high-fibre</b> diet.) Fibre slows the absorption of glucose into the blood, so blood sugar rises slowly without sudden insulin surges; it also fills you up and helps control weight, lowering the risk of type 2 diabetes. A low-fibre diet does the opposite.'}
]},
{t:'B4 – Animal systems, excretion, reproduction', qs:[
{n:'A1.',q:'Digestive systems I and II',a:'A. <b>Alimentary canal</b> (tube gut, e.g. earthworm).\nB. I has <b>two openings</b> (mouth and anus); II (coral polyp) has a <b>sac gut</b> with one opening.\nC. A tube gut lets food move one way through specialised regions (crop, gizzard, intestine) so digestion and absorption are continuous – efficient for an earthworm eating soil all the time. A sac gut takes food in and waste out through the same mouth, so feeding and digestion can\'t happen at once – enough for a slow, small polyp whose tentacles catch plankton in water.'},
{n:'A2.',q:'Circulatory systems 1 and 2; open system',a:'A. 1 = <b>open</b>; 2 = <b>closed</b>.\nB. E.g. closed system in an earthworm: blood stays inside vessels, pumped by "hearts" (aortic arches), and exchanges materials with tissues through capillaries. (Open, e.g. grasshopper: heart pumps haemolymph into sinuses that bathe the organs.)\nC. Small animals have a large SA : V ratio and short distances, so diffusion and slow, low-pressure flow can supply their cells; insects also get O₂ from the tracheal system, not the blood.'},
{n:'B1–2.',q:'Nitrogenous wastes; how one is excreted',a:'1. A. Human: <b>urea</b>. B. Insect: <b>uric acid</b>.\n⚠ The MOE scheme also accepts ammonia for humans; humans excrete urea, so ammonia is wrong.\n2. Human: the liver turns excess amino acids into urea; the <b>kidneys</b> filter the blood and reabsorb useful substances and water; urine passes down the ureters to the bladder and out through the urethra. (Insect: <b>Malpighian tubules</b> take wastes from haemolymph into the gut; water is reabsorbed in the rectum and dry uric acid is passed with faeces – saves water on land.)'},
{n:'B3–4.',q:'Missing terms; foetus A, B; placenta; dissection',a:'3. A = <b>fertilisation</b>; B = <b>implantation</b>.\n4A. A = <b>amniotic fluid/amnion</b>; B = <b>umbilical cord</b>.\nB. In the placenta, mother\'s and foetus\'s blood come close but do not mix. Glucose, amino acids, vitamins, minerals and O₂ <b>diffuse</b> (or are actively transported) from mother to foetus across villi with a large surface area; CO₂ and urea pass back to the mother.\nC. Wear gloves; pin the animal on its back on a dissecting board; lift the skin with forceps and cut along the mid-ventral line with scissors (not too deep); pin back the skin and muscle; move organs carefully with a blunt probe; draw and label; dispose of the animal and wash hands.'}
]},
{t:'B5 Part A – Adaptation and population', qs:[
{n:'1–3.',q:'Gause\'s principle; niche; types of adaptation with local examples',a:'1. (i) Competitive exclusion: two species competing for exactly the same limited resources cannot coexist – one wins and the other dies out or moves. (ii) Niche = the role of a species in its ecosystem: what it eats, where/when it lives and how it interacts.\n2. <b>Structural</b>, <b>behavioural</b>, <b>physiological</b> (any two).\n3. Structural: pandanus has prop roots for loose sandy soil; frigate birds have long wings for gliding over the ocean. Behavioural: crabs hide in burrows in the heat of the day; bonefish come in with the tide to feed. Physiological: mangroves excrete excess salt through their leaves.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Survivorship curves: bird, human',a:'A. (i) Bird: <b>Type II (B)</b>. (ii) Human: <b>Type I (A)</b>.\nB. (i) Birds die at a roughly constant rate at all ages (predation, accidents), so the line falls steadily. (ii) Humans have few offspring with lots of parental care and medicine, so most survive to old age and die late in life.'},
{n:'5–6.',q:'Genetic diversity; sampling methods',a:'5. A population with many different alleles has some individuals that can survive new diseases or environmental changes, so the population survives; low diversity (inbreeding, small isolated populations) increases harmful recessive traits and extinction risk.\n6. <b>Quadrat</b>: place square frames at random, count individuals or % cover in each, then scale up to the whole area. <b>Line/belt transect</b>: run a tape across the area and record organisms touching the line (or in quadrats placed along it) – shows changes along a gradient.'}
]},
{t:'B5 Part B – Succession and coral ecosystems', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Groups at times 1 and 3; roles; competition',a:'A. 1 = <b>pioneer species</b> (lichens, mosses); 3 = <b>climax community</b> (large trees).\nB. Pioneers colonise bare rock, break it down and add organic matter when they die, forming soil for later species. The climax community is the stable, final community that stays unless disturbed.\nC. Competition for food or space lowers the numbers or growth of the weaker competitor; it may be pushed out or forced to use a different niche. Intraspecific competition controls population size.'},
{n:'2–3.',q:'Coral: biotic and abiotic; zonation; distribution',a:'2A. (i) Biotic: coral polyps, fish, seagrass, turtles. (ii) Abiotic: sunlight, temperature, salinity, wave action.\n⚠ The MOE scheme lists the two the wrong way round.\nB. Light decreases with depth (zooxanthellae need light), so corals are richest in shallow clear water; strong waves on the reef crest favour short, solid corals; sheltered lagoons have branching corals; exposure at low tide limits corals on the reef flat. These create zones: reef flat → crest → slope.\n3. Corals are not spread evenly: they are clumped in zones that suit them – massive and encrusting corals on the windward reef crest, branching corals in the calm lagoon/back reef, fewer and flatter corals down the deep slope.'}
]},
{t:'Section C – Extended responses', qs:[
{n:'C1.',q:'Four community interactions with examples and factors',a:'Plan (10 marks): Introduction – species in a community interact.\n<b>Predation</b>: predator kills and eats prey (shark and reef fish); controls prey numbers.\n<b>Parasitism</b> (+/−): parasite lives on/in a host and harms it (tapeworm in humans, lice, fish lice).\n<b>Mutualism</b> (+/+): both benefit (coral polyp and zooxanthellae; clownfish and anemone; lichen).\n<b>Commensalism</b> (+/0): one benefits, the other is unaffected (remora on a shark; barnacles on a whale).\nFactors: food and space availability, population density, season/climate, human disturbance. Conclusion: these interactions keep populations balanced.'},
{n:'C2.',q:'Significance of biodiversity for an ecosystem',a:'Plan (10 marks): Introduction – biodiversity = variety of genes, species and ecosystems.\nSpecies richness and genetic diversity make an ecosystem stable and able to recover.\nSpecies depend on each other (pollination, coral–algae, food).\nFood chains and webs: many links mean that if one species is lost, others have alternative food; energy flows from producers to consumers.\nThreats: humans (overfishing, pollution, sand mining, clearing mangroves) and natural events (cyclones, coral bleaching from warming) reduce biodiversity.\nConclusion: protecting biodiversity protects food, income and the whole ecosystem.'}
]}
]},
{y:2022, src:'P2022/KSSC BIOLOGY 2022.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the MOE solutions. The official multiple-choice key has many errors; the correct answers are given here and ⚠ marks them.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–10',q:'',a:'1 <b>B</b> speeds up reactions (D is wrong – enzymes <i>lower</i> activation energy) · 2 <b>D</b> V points to the chloroplast · 3 <b>C</b> · 4 <b>A</b> osmosis · 5 <b>B</b> · 6 <b>B</b> DNA · 7 <b>A</b> metaphase · 8 <b>C</b> co-dominance (both colours show) · 9 <b>D</b> · 10 <b>A</b>\n⚠ The MOE key gives 7 B (interphase) and 8 B (recessive alleles), which are wrong.'},
{n:'11–20',q:'',a:'11 <b>C</b> absorption · 12 <b>B</b> cellular respiration · 13 <b>C</b> · 14 <b>A</b> homeostasis · 15 <b>C</b> prepares the uterus for implantation · 16 <b>A</b> worm · 17 <b>D</b> physiological · 18 <b>C</b> birth rate · 19 <b>A</b> climax · 20 <b>B</b> food web\n⚠ The MOE key gives 12 A, 13 A, 15 B, 18 D and 19 B, which are wrong.'}
]},
{t:'B1 – Cellular biology', qs:[
{n:'A.',q:'Chloroplast: process, energy, equation, two phases',a:'a. <b>Photosynthesis</b>.\nb. Converts <b>light energy</b> into <b>chemical energy</b> stored in glucose.\nc. 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ (light, chlorophyll)\nd. The <b>light-dependent stage</b> (in the <b>thylakoids/grana</b>) traps light, splits water and makes ATP and NADPH. The <b>light-independent stage/Calvin cycle</b> (in the <b>stroma</b>) uses that ATP and NADPH to fix CO₂ into glucose. Separating them lets each set of enzymes work in its own place, and the second needs the products of the first.'},
{n:'B.',q:'Respiration',a:'a. C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O (+ about 38 ATP)\nb. X = <b>glycolysis</b> (cytoplasm); Y = <b>Krebs cycle</b> (mitochondrial matrix); Z = <b>electron transport chain</b> (inner membrane/cristae).\nc. Energy is released <b>step by step</b> so it can be captured as ATP instead of lost as heat; each stage uses the products of the one before.\nd. (i) Animals: <b>lactic acid</b> (+ a little ATP). (ii) Plants/yeast: <b>ethanol and carbon dioxide</b>.\n2. Yeast respires anaerobically (fermentation): glucose → ethanol + CO₂. In bread the CO₂ bubbles make the dough rise (ethanol evaporates in baking); in beer the ethanol is kept and the CO₂ makes it fizzy.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Guard cells; isotonic/hypertonic/hypotonic',a:'1. When water enters the guard cells they become <b>turgid</b> and bend apart (thicker inner wall) – the stoma opens and water vapour can leave. When they lose water they become <b>flaccid</b> and the stoma closes, saving water.\n2. A: <b>isotonic</b> – same concentration; no net water movement; cell unchanged.\nB: <b>hypertonic</b> (solution more concentrated) – water leaves by osmosis; cell shrinks/crenates (plant cell plasmolysed).\nC: <b>hypotonic</b> (solution more dilute) – water enters; animal cell swells and may burst (plant cell turgid).\n(Match the labels to the pictures: the shrunken cell is in the hypertonic beaker; the swollen cell is in the hypotonic one.)'},
{n:'D.',q:'Plant vs animal cell; wet mount',a:'1. Plant cells have a <b>cell wall</b>, <b>chloroplasts</b> and a large permanent <b>vacuole</b>; animal cells do not (they are also irregular in shape).\n2. (1) Place a thin specimen in a drop of water (or stain) on the centre of a clean slide. (2) Lower a coverslip at 45° slowly with a needle to avoid air bubbles. (3) Blot off excess water, then view under low power first.'}
]},
{t:'B2 – Genetics', qs:[
{n:'A.',q:'Gene, protein synthesis, polypeptide; two processes',a:'1. A gene is a section of DNA whose base sequence codes for one <b>polypeptide</b>. In protein synthesis the code is copied and read three bases (a codon) at a time; each codon specifies one amino acid, which are joined into a polypeptide chain that folds into the protein.\n2. <b>Transcription</b> – in the nucleus the gene is copied into mRNA. <b>Translation</b> – at a ribosome, tRNA brings amino acids matching each mRNA codon and they are joined into a polypeptide.'},
{n:'B.',q:'Mitosis vs meiosis; sources of variation',a:'1. Mitosis: same chromosome number (diploid 2n); 2 daughter cells; identical (no variation); growth, repair, asexual reproduction.\nMeiosis: half the number (haploid n); 4 daughter cells; all different; makes gametes.\n2. <b>Crossing over</b>: in prophase I homologous chromosomes swap sections, making new allele combinations on each chromosome. <b>Recombination</b>: these new combinations end up in the gametes. <b>Independent assortment</b>: homologous pairs line up randomly at metaphase I, so each gamete gets a random mix of maternal and paternal chromosomes.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Multiple alleles; avocado genotypes; test cross',a:'1. A gene with <b>more than two alleles</b> in the population (each person still has only two), e.g. ABO blood groups (Iᴬ, Iᴮ, i).\n2a. P1 = <b>RRSS</b>; P2 = <b>rrss</b> (all offspring RrSs – round, smooth).\nb. A <b>test cross</b>: mate the black dog with a white (homozygous recessive) dog. If any white puppies appear, the black dog is Bb; if all are black (in a large litter), it is BB.'}
]},
{t:'B3 – Plants', qs:[
{n:'A.',q:'Vascular tissue, root hairs, transpiration, stomata',a:'1. Monocots: vascular bundles <b>scattered</b> through the stem, no cambium. Dicots: bundles arranged in a <b>ring</b>, with cambium between xylem (inside) and phloem (outside).\n2. a. <b>xylem</b> b. <b>phloem</b>\n3. Root hairs are long thin extensions of epidermal cells that give a huge <b>surface area</b> and thin walls for absorbing water (osmosis) and minerals (active transport, using ATP).\n4. Wind blows away the humid air around the leaf, keeping a steep water-vapour gradient, so transpiration is <b>faster</b> (also: higher temperature or light → faster; humidity → slower).\n5. Stomata let CO₂ in and O₂ and water vapour out. Guard cells open the pore when turgid (in light) and close it when flaccid (dark or water shortage).'},
{n:'B.',q:'Fern life cycle',a:'a. Haploid (A): spores, prothallus/gametophyte, gametes. Diploid (B): zygote, young and mature fern (sporophyte), sporangia.\nb. Ferns need a film of <b>water</b> for the sperm to swim to the egg, and it is a single fertilisation. Angiosperms use <b>pollen tubes</b> (no water needed) and have <b>double fertilisation</b> (zygote + endosperm).\nc. Offspring are genetically <b>identical</b> – no variation, so a disease or change in the environment can wipe them all out; overcrowding and competition with the parent.'},
{n:'C.',q:'Diet and health; liver and blood sugar',a:'1a. Low fibre: food moves slowly → <b>constipation</b>, higher risk of bowel cancer, obesity and diabetes.\nb. Lots of spicy food irritates the stomach lining → heartburn/indigestion and can make <b>ulcers</b> worse.\nc. High cholesterol → fatty deposits in arteries (atherosclerosis) → high blood pressure, <b>heart disease</b>, stroke; gallstones.\n2. When blood glucose is high, insulin makes the liver take up glucose and store it as <b>glycogen</b>; when low, glucagon makes the liver break glycogen down to glucose. In <b>diabetes</b> there is too little insulin (or cells don\'t respond), so the liver doesn\'t store glucose and blood sugar stays high.\n⚠ The MOE scheme describes the kidneys, but the question asks about the liver.'},
{n:'D.',q:'Digestive processes; type 1 gut',a:'1. Ingestion – taking food into the mouth. Digestion – breaking large insoluble food into small soluble molecules (mechanical and chemical, by enzymes). Absorption – digested molecules pass through the gut wall into the blood. Egestion – removing undigested waste (faeces) through the anus.\n2. Type 1 = <b>sac gut</b>, e.g. coral polyp/sea anemone/Hydra: tentacles push food into the single mouth; gland cells release enzymes into the gastrovascular cavity; cells lining it absorb (and engulf) the food; undigested waste goes back out through the <b>same mouth</b>.'},
{n:'E.',q:'Breathing vs gas exchange vs respiration; four animals',a:'1. Breathing (ventilation) = moving air/water in and out of the gas exchange surface. Gas exchange = diffusion of O₂ in and CO₂ out across that surface. Cellular respiration = the chemical reaction in cells that releases energy from glucose using O₂.\n2a. Earthworm: through its <b>moist skin</b> by diffusion; mucus keeps it moist; capillaries under the skin carry gases.\nb. Fish: water enters the mouth and passes over the <b>gills</b> (filaments with thin lamellae and many capillaries); <b>counter-current flow</b> (blood and water in opposite directions) keeps a gradient; water leaves under the operculum.\nc. Cockroach: air enters through <b>spiracles</b> into <b>tracheae</b> and tiny tracheoles that reach the cells directly; body movements pump air.\nd. Humans: air is breathed into the lungs (diaphragm and rib muscles); O₂ diffuses from the <b>alveoli</b> into the capillaries and CO₂ the other way; alveoli are thin, moist and have a huge surface area.'},
{n:'F.',q:'Circulation',a:'a. Humans are large, active and warm-blooded, with a small SA : V ratio, so diffusion is too slow; a heart and blood vessels carry O₂ and food quickly to every cell.\nb. Open: blood (haemolymph) is pumped out of vessels into body cavities and bathes the organs directly; low pressure. Closed: blood stays inside vessels all the time; high pressure and faster flow.\nc. Fish: closed, single circulation (heart → gills → body) – efficient enough for a fairly large active animal supported by water. Insect: open circulation, slow and low pressure, but enough because insects are small and get O₂ from tracheae, not blood.\nd. Smoking narrows and damages arteries and raises blood pressure; heavy alcohol raises blood pressure and fat in the blood; obesity raises cholesterol and blood pressure. Fatty plaque builds up in the coronary arteries, blocking blood to the heart muscle → angina/heart attack.'},
{n:'G–H.',q:'Excretion; kidney stones and gout; implantation; placenta',a:'G1. Fish excrete <b>ammonia</b> through the <b>gills</b> – toxic but very soluble, and they have plenty of water to dilute it. Insects excrete dry <b>uric acid</b> via <b>Malpighian tubules</b> – non-toxic and saves water on land.\n2. Kidney stones: minerals (calcium salts) crystallise in the kidney → pain and blocked urine flow. Gout: too much uric acid in the blood forms crystals in joints → painful, swollen joints.\nH1. Ovulation → fertilisation in the oviduct → the zygote divides (morula) as it moves down → blastocyst reaches the uterus, whose lining has been thickened by progesterone → it sinks into the lining (<b>implantation</b>).\n2. In the placenta: <b>O₂</b> diffuses to the foetus and <b>CO₂</b> back; <b>nutrients</b> (glucose, amino acids) pass to the foetus; <b>wastes</b> such as urea pass to the mother; some <b>antibodies</b> cross, giving immunity; but harmful <b>drugs</b>, alcohol and nicotine can also cross and damage the baby.'}
]},
{t:'B4 – Environment', qs:[
{n:'A.',q:'Classification; key',a:'1. Shared features such as body symmetry, number of cell layers, type of body cavity, presence of a backbone, segmentation, type of skeleton, how they reproduce.\n2. Kingdom → phylum → class → order → family → genus → species.\n3. A = <b>Magnolia</b> (simple, smooth edge); B = <b>Walnut</b> (compound, leaflets at many points); C = <b>Elm</b> (simple, small tooth-like edge).\n4. G (Chestnut): compound leaf with all leaflets joined at <b>one point</b>. H (Holly): simple leaf, jagged edge with <b>large thorny teeth</b>. F (White Oak): simple, smooth-edged but <b>lobed</b>.'},
{n:'B.',q:'Types of adaptation; Gause and Liebig',a:'1. a. Octopus camouflage – <b>physiological</b> (colour change by pigment cells) b. spider spinning a web – <b>behavioural</b> c. polar bear\'s thick fur – <b>structural</b>.\n2. Gause\'s principle: two species can\'t share exactly the same niche, so species adapt to use different resources. Liebig\'s law: growth is limited by the scarcest factor, so organisms adapt to survive the limiting factor (e.g. plants in dry places store water).'},
{n:'C.',q:'Survivorship curves; variation; isolation',a:'1. Type 1: low mortality when young, most die in old age; low natality and lots of parental care (humans, elephants).\nType 2: constant death rate at all ages (birds, lizards).\nType 3: very high death rate among the young, few survive; very high natality with no parental care (fish, coral, oysters).\n2. Variation means some individuals can survive diseases or environmental changes, so the population survives and can evolve.\n3. Isolated populations can\'t interbreed; gene flow stops, so they slowly become different (and may form new species); small isolated populations have low diversity and inbreeding.'},
{n:'D–E.',q:'Pioneers; stratification; relationships; pyramid; local issue',a:'D1. Pioneers colonise bare areas and start soil formation, making conditions suitable for later species.\n2. Stratification = vertical layering of a community (canopy, understorey, shrub and ground layers).\n3. Predation: one animal kills and eats another (shark eats reef fish). Commensalism: one benefits, the other is unaffected (remora on a shark; epiphytes on trees). Mutualism: both benefit (coral and zooxanthellae; lichen). Parasitism: one benefits, the host is harmed (tapeworm in pigs/humans; lice).\nE1. Energy passes from producers to herbivores to carnivores; only about <b>10%</b> passes on at each level (the rest is lost as heat, movement, waste), so each level is smaller.\n2. E.g. coastal erosion/sand mining, rubbish (plastic) pollution, overfishing, coral bleaching.\n3. E.g. overfishing removes key species, breaking food chains; algae can overgrow reefs; fewer species → lower biodiversity, a less stable ecosystem and less food for people in the long term.'}
]},
{t:'B5 and Section C', qs:[
{n:'B5.',q:'Energy profile with and without enzyme',a:'a. Title: Effect of an enzyme on activation energy. Aim: to compare the activation energy of a reaction with and without an enzyme.\nb. The curve without enzyme has a much higher peak (activation energy) than the curve with enzyme; the energy of the reactants and products, and so the overall energy change, is the same for both.\nc. Enzymes speed up reactions by <b>lowering the activation energy</b>; they do not change the overall energy change.'},
{n:'C1.',q:'Essay: enzymes',a:'Plan (10 marks): Intro – enzymes are protein biological catalysts.\nPurpose: speed up reactions in cells at body temperature by lowering activation energy; not used up.\nSpecificity: active site fits only one substrate (lock and key).\nFactors: temperature (rises to an <b>optimum</b>, about 37 °C, then <b>denatured</b>); pH (each enzyme has an optimum pH – pepsin 2, amylase 7); substrate and enzyme concentration; inhibitors.\nDenaturing: high temperature or extreme pH changes the shape of the active site permanently.\nConclusion.'},
{n:'C2.',q:'Essay: selective breeding or genetic engineering',a:'Plan: Intro → Description: selective breeding – choose parents with desired traits and breed for many generations (e.g. high-yield taro, layer hens); genetic engineering – cut a gene from one organism and insert it into another (e.g. insulin-making bacteria, Bt cotton).\nAdvantages: higher yields, disease/pest resistance, better nutrition (golden rice), medicines.\nDisadvantages: reduced genetic variation (inbreeding, disease risk); ethical concerns; GM genes spreading to wild plants; cost.\nConclusion: useful, but must be managed carefully.'}
]}
]},
{y:2021, src:'P2021/KSSC Biology 2021.pdf', note:'Full worked solutions. No MOE marking scheme was published for this year, so these answers were prepared from the prescription and the figures in the paper.',
secs:[
{t:'Section A – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–10',q:'',a:'1 <b>D</b> salt ions moving into cells are usually actively transported · 2 <b>A</b> nucleus · 3 <b>A</b> electron transport chain · 4 <b>D</b> active site · 5 <b>D</b> deoxyribose + phosphate + cytosine · 6 <b>C</b> 4 cells, 10 chromosomes each · 7 <b>D</b> chlorophyll content · 8 <b>A</b> asexual offspring are identical, so they adapt poorly · 9 <b>A</b> · 10 <b>D</b> commensalism'},
{n:'11–20',q:'',a:'11 <b>C</b> about 90% of energy is lost at each level · 12 <b>A</b> the red fox is genus <i>Vulpes</i>, the others <i>Canis</i> · 13 <b>D</b> renal vein (kidney) · 14 <b>B</b> AO × BO can give A, B and O children · 15 <b>C</b> body surface · 16 <b>C</b> oestrogen repairs/rebuilds the uterus lining (progesterone maintains it) · 17 <b>C</b> letter A – the home range (large outer area) · 18 <b>B</b> · 19 <b>D</b> behavioural · 20 <b>B</b>'}
]},
{t:'B1 – Cellular biology', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Dialysis bags (bag/beaker): 1 0.2 M/0.6 M, 2 0.2/1.0, 3 0.2/0.2, 4 1.0/0.2',a:'a. <b>Beaker 3</b> – the concentration inside and outside is the same (0.2 M), so there is no net movement of water.\nb. <b>Bags 1 and 2</b> – the solution outside is more concentrated (hypertonic), so water leaves the bags by osmosis.\nc. In beaker 4 the bag (1.0 M) is more concentrated than the beaker (0.2 M), so water moves <b>into</b> the bag by osmosis – its mass <b>increases</b> and it swells (it could burst).'},
{n:'2.',q:'Animal cell diagram',a:'a. B: <b>Golgi body</b>. D: <b>mitochondrion</b>. (C is the cell membrane.)\nb. A – <b>endoplasmic reticulum</b>: a network of membranes that transports materials through the cell; rough ER (with ribosomes) makes and carries proteins.\nc. <b>Animal</b> cell – no cell wall, no chloroplasts, no large central vacuole, rounded irregular shape.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Light-dependent reactions',a:'a. The <b>thylakoid membranes (grana)</b>.\nb. X = <b>water</b> (H₂O) (or light energy/ADP).\nc. The light phase uses light energy to make <b>ATP</b> (energy) and split water to give <b>hydrogen</b> (carried by NADPH). The dark phase (Calvin cycle) needs this ATP and hydrogen to reduce CO₂ to <b>glucose</b>, so it cannot happen without the light phase.\nd. 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ (light, chlorophyll)'},
{n:'4.',q:'Stages of respiration',a:'a. <b>Glycolysis</b>.\nb. <b>Krebs cycle</b> (citric acid cycle) in the matrix.\nc. Stage 3 (electron transport chain): electrons from NADH/FADH₂ pass along carriers on the inner membrane, pumping H⁺; H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase making about 34 ATP; oxygen accepts electrons and H⁺ to form water.\nd. Plants (and yeast): <b>ethanol + carbon dioxide</b>. Animals: <b>lactic acid</b> only (no CO₂). Both make only 2 ATP.'},
{n:'5.',q:'pH and two proteases',a:'a. Enzyme A ≈ <b>pH 2</b>; Enzyme B ≈ <b>pH 8</b>.\nb. At pH 6, enzyme A has almost no activity (denatured), while enzyme B is working but below its optimum.\nc. The stomach is strongly <b>acidic</b> (about pH 2) – the stomach enzyme A works best at low pH (hydrochloric acid is made there).'}
]},
{t:'B2 – Genetics', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'ABO blood groups',a:'a. A recessive allele is only expressed when no dominant allele is present (homozygous, e.g. IᴼIᴼ).\nb. IᴬIᴮ × IᴼIᴼ → gametes Iᴬ, Iᴮ × Iᴼ → children IᴬIᴼ (group <b>A</b>) and IᴮIᴼ (group <b>B</b>), 50% each.\nc. A group O child must be IᴼIᴼ – one Iᴼ from each parent. An AB father has no Iᴼ allele (he can only pass Iᴬ or Iᴮ), so he cannot be the father of a group O child.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Protein synthesis diagram',a:'a. X: <b>transcription</b>. Y: <b>translation</b>.\nb. <b>Ribosome</b>.\nc. <b>mRNA</b>.\nd. The structure is <b>tRNA</b>: it carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome and its anticodon pairs with the matching mRNA codon, so amino acids are joined in the right order.'},
{n:'3–4.',q:'GM: advantage and disadvantage; chromosomes, DNA, genes; variation; mitosis and meiosis',a:'3. Advantage: e.g. bacteria given the human insulin gene make cheap insulin for diabetics; pest-resistant (Bt) crops give higher yields with fewer pesticides. Disadvantage: GM genes may spread to wild plants; unknown long-term health/ecological effects; seed costs and ethical concerns.\n4a. Chromosomes are made of <b>DNA</b> coiled around proteins; a <b>gene</b> is a short section of DNA on a chromosome that codes for one protein. Each chromosome carries many genes.\nb. <b>Crossing over</b> (homologous chromosomes swap sections in meiosis I); <b>independent assortment</b> (random line-up of chromosome pairs); <b>random fertilisation</b> (any sperm with any egg) – also mutation. Each makes new combinations of alleles.\nc. Mitosis: growth, repair and asexual reproduction – identical diploid cells. Meiosis: makes haploid gametes so the chromosome number stays constant after fertilisation, and creates variation.'}
]},
{t:'B3 – Organisms', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Digestion; diets; liver; fibre; sea anemone vs earthworm',a:'a. A <b>ingestion</b> – taking food into the mouth. B digestion – breaking food down. C <b>absorption</b> – digested food passes into the blood through the small intestine. D egestion – removing undigested waste.\nb. E.g. instant noodles and white rice; soft drinks and fried/fast food (tinned meat). High fat and sugar → obesity, type 2 diabetes, heart disease and tooth decay.\nc. After a meal insulin makes the liver change excess glucose into <b>glycogen</b> to store; between meals glucagon makes it break glycogen back into glucose. In <b>diabetes</b> there is too little insulin (or the body ignores it), so glucose is not stored and blood sugar stays high.\nd. Little fibre → food moves slowly → <b>constipation</b>; increased risk of bowel cancer, piles and diverticulitis.\ne–f. Sea anemone: <b>sac gut</b> with one opening; tentacles with stinging cells catch prey; digestion in the gastrovascular cavity; waste out of the mouth – suits a slow, attached animal that catches occasional prey. Earthworm: <b>tube gut</b> (mouth to anus) with crop (store), gizzard (grind) and long intestine – continuous one-way processing of soil for organic matter, suits continuous feeding.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Menstrual cycle; placenta',a:'a. Day 5: <b>menstruation</b> ending/uterus lining starting to rebuild. Day 14: <b>ovulation</b> – an egg is released.\nb. Fertilisation: about <b>day 14–16</b>. Implantation: about <b>day 20–22</b> (about a week after fertilisation). Menstruation: <b>days 1–5</b>.\nc. The placenta brings the mother\'s and foetus\'s blood close (without mixing): <b>O₂</b> diffuses in and <b>CO₂</b> out; <b>nutrients</b> (glucose, amino acids) pass to the foetus; <b>wastes</b> (urea) pass to the mother; some <b>antibodies</b> cross, giving immunity; harmful <b>drugs</b>, alcohol and nicotine can also cross and damage the foetus.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Insect gas exchange',a:'a. Tracheae branch into huge numbers of very thin <b>tracheoles</b> that reach every muscle fibre, giving a large surface area and short diffusion distance (ends filled with fluid).\nb. Insects take <b>air</b> straight to cells through tubes (no blood involved); fish take O₂ from <b>water</b> over gills and the blood carries it.\nc. Breathing = moving air/water to the gas exchange surface. Gas exchange = diffusion of O₂ and CO₂ across the surface. Cellular respiration = the reaction in cells releasing energy from glucose using O₂.\nd. Earthworm: gases diffuse across the thin, <b>moist skin</b> (kept moist by mucus) into capillaries just below it; haemoglobin carries O₂.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Human circulatory system',a:'a. Humans: <b>closed</b> (blood always in vessels) and <b>double</b> circulation (through the heart twice), with a 4-chambered heart and haemoglobin carrying O₂. Insects: open circulation – haemolymph bathes the organs; a simple tube heart; blood does not carry O₂.\nb. The <b>aorta</b> (leaving the left ventricle).\nc. Higher pressure → faster delivery; blood can be directed to the organs that need it most; oxygenated and deoxygenated blood are kept separate.\nd. Closed circulation suits large, active animals that need lots of O₂ quickly; an open system is slow and low-pressure, enough only for small animals (or those using tracheae).\ne. Smoking damages artery walls, raises blood pressure and makes blood clot; obesity raises cholesterol and blood pressure. Fatty plaque narrows the coronary arteries, so heart muscle gets less O₂ → angina or heart attack.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Dicot stem, phloem, stomata, root hair',a:'a. The vascular bundles are arranged in a <b>ring</b>.\nb. Phloem carries dissolved sugars (sucrose) and amino acids from the leaves to growing parts and storage organs (translocation), both up and down.\nc. Stomata let CO₂ in for photosynthesis and O₂ and water vapour out. When guard cells take in water they become turgid and curve apart – the stoma opens; when they lose water they become flaccid – it closes.\nd. The root hair is a long, thin extension of one cell that gives a large surface area; its thin wall and membrane let water enter by osmosis (sap is more concentrated than soil water) and minerals by active transport (mitochondria provide ATP).'}
]},
{t:'B4 – Skills', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Mass potometer (87.63 g at 9:07 am → 83.02 g at 3:36 pm)',a:'a. A (weighing/mass) <b>potometer</b> on an electronic balance.\nb. 87.63 − 83.02 = <b>4.61 g</b>\nc. Time = 6 h 29 min = 389 min → rate = 4.61/389 = <b>0.0119 g/min</b> (≈ 0.71 g/h)\nd. Light intensity, temperature, humidity or wind.\ne. Repeat with several plants and average; keep other conditions constant; use a layer of oil to stop evaporation from the water surface; take readings at regular intervals.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Onion cell slide; staining; magnification',a:'a. (i) Peel a thin layer of epidermis from inside an onion scale with forceps. (ii) Lay it flat in a drop of water/iodine on a clean slide. (iii) Lower a coverslip at 45° with a needle to avoid air bubbles. (iv) View on low power first, focus, then switch to high power.\nb. Stain (iodine) makes the nucleus and cell walls visible by adding contrast.\nc. 10 × 10 = <b>×100</b>'}
]},
{t:'B5 – Environment', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Dichotomous key',a:'A <b>Chestnut</b> · B <b>Pine</b> · C <b>Magnolia</b> · D <b>Holly</b> · E <b>White Oak</b> · F <b>Spruce</b> · G <b>Walnut</b> · H <b>Elm</b>'},
{n:'2.',q:'Food web',a:'a. Primary: rabbit, mouse or grasshopper. Secondary: fox, owl or bird.\nb. Birds (and owls/foxes that eat them) would have less food and could decline; plants eaten by grasshoppers would increase; other consumers might eat more mice instead.\nc. Grass → grasshopper → bird → owl (arrows show the direction of energy flow).\nd. More species means more food links, so if one species is lost others can replace it – a diverse ecosystem is more stable and recovers better from disturbance.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Community pattern; succession; pioneers; competition',a:'a. <b>Ecological succession</b> (primary succession from bare ground to forest).\nb. The gradual, ordered change in the species making up a community over time, until a stable climax community forms.\nc. Pioneers (lichens, mosses, grasses) colonise bare areas, break down rock and add organic matter when they die, forming soil and making it suitable for other species.\nd. Species competing for space, food and nutrients reduce each other\'s growth; the stronger competitor may exclude the weaker or force it into a different niche; it controls population sizes.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Survivorship; variation; adaptation; Gause and Liebig; mutualism',a:'a. The number (or proportion) of individuals still alive at each age.\nb. Type II: a <b>constant</b> death rate at all ages (straight line).\nc. With little variation, all individuals react the same way to a new disease or environmental change, so they may all die; inbreeding also increases harmful recessive traits.\nd. Features (structural, physiological or behavioural) that help an organism survive and reproduce in its environment.\ne. Gause\'s principle: two species can\'t share the same niche – one outcompetes the other. Liebig\'s law: growth is controlled by the scarcest resource (the limiting factor).\nf. A relationship in which <b>both</b> species benefit (e.g. coral and zooxanthellae).'}
]},
{t:'Section C – Essays', qs:[
{n:'C1.',q:'Excretion',a:'Plan: CO₂ and water come from <b>cellular respiration</b>; nitrogenous waste comes from <b>deamination</b> of excess amino acids in the liver (ammonia → urea).\nFish excrete <b>ammonia</b> through the gills – very toxic but soluble, plenty of water to dilute it. Insects excrete <b>uric acid</b> via Malpighian tubules – almost dry, saves water on land. Humans excrete <b>urea</b> via the kidneys (filtration, reabsorption) – moderately toxic, needs some water.\nKidney stones: minerals crystallise in the kidney → pain, blocked urine flow, infection. Gout: excess uric acid forms crystals in joints → painful swollen joints.'},
{n:'C2.',q:'Fern vs moss life cycles',a:'Plan: both show <b>alternation of generations</b> and need water for sperm to swim to the egg.\nMoss: the <b>gametophyte</b> (haploid, green leafy plant) is dominant; the sporophyte is a small stalk and capsule that grows on and depends on the gametophyte.\nFern: the <b>sporophyte</b> (diploid fern with fronds, roots and vascular tissue) is dominant and independent; the gametophyte is a small heart-shaped prothallus.\nBoth: sporophyte makes spores by <b>meiosis</b>; spores grow into gametophytes by <b>mitosis</b>; gametophytes make gametes by mitosis; <b>fertilisation</b> forms the diploid zygote, which grows into a new sporophyte.'}
]}
]}
]});
